    ('162', "PROGRESS 2026-11-21 night: the title film synced once at the start and then only for 0.6 s+ drift, at most every 30 s (each jump showed as a flash) - watch a normal start. - TOMORROW THIRD (owner, 2026-11-20) - The title screen flashing has returned: check a normal start (not only the test runs) and fix. ALSO (found 11-20 night): in many test runs the menus' page never reported 'menus ready' (CEF slow / timing out while Chablais loads), so the start's violet cover (ADPHUD, layer 50) stayed up - may be the same cause as the flash; the test tours now skip that cover (written 11-20, compile tomorrow).", '1-2 h', ['none'], '', False),
    ('163', "DONE 2026-11-21 night (updater: curl to disk, never starts over, finished packages kept - built) - TOMORROW FOURTH (owner, 2026-11-20) - Rebuild the updater's downloading. STANDING RULE: every download in the game resumes where it stopped and never starts over - even if the game is closed in the middle of an update or a pack download. Several connections at once, every piece saved to disk as it arrives, each finished file checked against its fingerprint and only a damaged file fetched again. Today the updater uses one connection, holds the whole update in memory and restarts from zero on any break (Updater.cpp). Same rule for the terrain and the Chablais photo packs (28 GB).", '3-5 h', ['none'], '', True),
    ('154', "NUMBER 1 (owner, 2026-11-20) - ALL the AI of the game, complete, built from scratch in Unreal: every AI-related function the Babylon version has - the AI airlines and their own schedules, fleets and economy, everything they simulate, every option the NPCs have and every way you interact with them - understood first (what the Babylon version did, simulated and offered), then made new in Unreal Engine from scratch: never ported or copied over from Babylon. Everything as the owner has described he wants the game (his rules and plans in memory and docs), every single feature of the AI logic and how they operate. Plan document first for his OK, then built in parts.", 'many days (plan first)', ['none'], '', False),
    ('150', "TO DO (owner, 2026-11-20) - The terminal is not drawn from some camera angles: in the test runs only its roof showed from some views while it was there when walking - find why (bounds, culling, Nanite or streaming) and fix; check the stress walks were not measured with it missing.", '1-3 h', ['gfx'], '', False),
    ('120', 'TOP 6 TODAY No. 5 (owner, 2026-11-18; do not build or publish until he says) - The game UI hides while you hold your phone or tablet: when the phone or the tablet is in your hand (held, and up close too), all of the GAME UI disappears (the top bar, the map, the weather bar, notes, the floor plan in the corner and the rest - NOT the device own screen); the ONLY game UI that stays is the legend in the bottom right corner (BUTTONS F1). As soon as you put the phone or tablet away, all the game UI comes back. While holding it you can move the mouse freely, away from the device, and click the legend - add that if it is not possible now; and at the last step, up close with its screen open (E), the legend can be clicked too.', '2-3 h', ['none'], '', False),
    ('147', 'NUMBER 2 (owner, 2026-11-19; do not fix yet) - The zebra crossings are missing their texture: the white bars show plain (RoadNetwork.cpp zebra crossings: their material/texture not applied - check the paint material and its UVs on the crossing bars). Check the rest of the forecourt and roads for anything else missing its texture while at it (owner: "maybe something else is missing as well") and list what was found. ALSO (owner, same day): at NIGHT all the road textures seem to be missing - the roads, markings and crossings look untextured after dark (check the road materials under night lighting: the lamps, exposure, the textures themselves).', '2-3 h', ['none'], '', False),
    ('121', 'STANDING RULE for every stress test (owner, 2026-11-18) - only ONE copy of the game runs while testing: either started from the builder (the Unreal editor) or the normal installed game, never both at once (two copies drag the frame rate down and the numbers are wrong). The builder itself is never closed - it stays on. Tools/perf_walk.sh checks it before every walk: the editor window may stay open; any other running game copy stops the walk until it has ended.', 'standing rule', ['none'], '', False),
    ('122', "LATER (owner, 2026-11-18; not now) - The phone like a real phone: calls and messages. Calling plays a calling tune, an incoming call a different ringtone; messages their own tones; in the phone Settings: choose the tunes - about 20 for incoming calls, 20 for calling out, and the same for messages - and a theme. Call someone on the contact list: the game's voice AI makes them audible - you hear them talk - and you answer by choosing from creative options in a speech bubble, as when you stand in front of someone (the same kind of choices as talking face to face), never the same every time. Sometimes nobody answers (busy): an automatic answer - busy, call later, or leave a message after the tone, then a beep. To be improved when the time comes.", '1.5-2.5 days (rough, in parts)', ['none'], '', False),
    ('123', "LATER (owner, 2026-11-18) - Camera, video and gallery on the phone: a Camera app - the phone moves up into a position where you point it at what you want and hold it straight; portrait or landscape (the phone turned, the photo taken that way); modes that really work: photo, portrait, panorama and a few more; a video recorder; a Gallery app with what you took. Every photo and video is stored locally on the player's own PC.", '1.5-2 days (rough)', ['none'], '', False),
    ('124', 'LATER (owner, 2026-11-18) - Weather app on the phone, the tablet AND the computers: a full weather app from the real-world weather that already drives the game - the forecast for the day by time of day (rain, snow, storm, thunderstorm, sun, partly sunny, broken clouds and the rest), temperature, wind strength and the direction it comes from - useful in your work at the airport (e.g. which runway, de-icing, ground work).', '1-1.5 days', ['none'], '', False),
    ('125', 'LATER (owner, 2026-11-18) - Vehicles on wet, snowy and soft ground: every vehicle on wet tarmac (raining or after rain) throws a fine spray off the back of its tyres as real cars do; through a puddle a bigger splash, on the side whose wheels touch it, at the moment they do; on snow, tyre tracks pressed into the snow (not rubber marks); on grass a normal car leaves light pressed marks, doughnuts on grass send grass flying and in time make the car muddy and wear the grass out; doughnuts on tarmac give smoke and rubber marks; heavy vehicles (the fire truck) leave deep tracks and deform the ground (as talked about before), and the deformed ground heals back to normal over about 3 real days. Driving off-road: stone chips, gravel and mud heard hitting the underside. Puddles show rings of ripples spreading out further and further when a wheel hits them - and when you walk through them. PERFORMANCE (owner): stunning but light - every effect costs only while it is triggered (a wheel in a puddle, driving on a big wet surface, a doughnut on grass), nothing runs otherwise.', '2-3 days (rough, in parts)', ['none'], '', False),
    ('126', 'LATER (owner, 2026-11-18) - Pushing doors open with your hand: walking up to a door that simply swings open when pushed (no latch or lock holding it), your right arm comes up a little and your right palm presses on the door as it gives way - for every push door (a hand animation, made for it).', '3-5 h', ['none'], '', False),
    ('127', 'LATER (owner, 2026-11-18; with 116 staff daily life) - Dirty work clothes: mechanics and crew who have worked on an aircraft in the hangar, maintained a jetway or done other dirty jobs get visibly dirty uniforms / work clothing (grease, dust, wet), cleaned when they change or go home.', '4-6 h', ['none'], '', False),
    ('128', "LATER (owner, 2026-11-18; with 69a people talk) - Your own voice in dialogue: what you choose to say to people face to face is spoken aloud in YOUR voice - a woman's voice if you play a woman, a man's if a man (on phone calls this already works).", '2-3 h', ['none'], '', False),
    ('116', 'BUILD FIRST (before the crew use the jetway HMI) - Staff live their own lives: home, commute, 8-hour shifts (owner, 2026-11-18). Every member of your staff - gate agents and all the rest of the crew - is a real person in the world with their own life: (1) HOME: each lives in a house or flat in the same town as you (the town is coming later - its homes are the places this system uses; until it is built, a placeholder home address per person in the town area); (2) GETTING TO WORK: before their shift they leave home and come to the airport by their own car (drives the real roads, parks in the staff car park) or by bus (walks to a stop, rides the bus, walks in) - never appearing out of nowhere; (3) CLOCKING IN: they walk in through the staff entrance, change if their job has a uniform, and go to where their duty is; (4) THE SHIFT: an 8-hour shift per agent, from a rota (early / day / late / night, days off, holidays, sick days), following their duty list for that shift - a gate agent walks to the gate and the jetway console about 20 minutes before an arrival, does the T-10 jetway preparation on the HMI, docks it only when the aircraft is parked with engines and beacon off, undocks before departure; breaks on the shift; (5) GOING HOME: when the 8 hours are over they hand over to the next shift, clock out, change, walk out to their car or the bus stop and go home; (6) the rota makes sure every duty is covered at every hour (a missing person = a late flight); the same daily life later for every job (ground crew, fire crew, security, shops, cleaners ...). Needs: a person record (name, home, job, shift pattern, car or bus), the rota, the route planner home <-> airport (roads, bus line), the staff car park and staff entrance, and the duty system the jetway HMI, boarding and deboarding hang on.', '3-5 days (rough, to be split into parts)', ['none'], '', False),
    ('115', 'NEXT - Jetway HMI: the console screen as a real working control screen for its own jetway (owner, 2026-11-18; plan docs/Jetway-HMI-Plan.md): outline + E -> camera up close (no clipping) -> its own web UI (same system as the PC monitors); Position (live), Pre-positioning per aircraft type/door, Cameras (door, bogie, cab floor), Auto-levelling, System status, Safety, Alarms + history, Maintenance (a system check that runs), Settings, Login roles; DOCK/UNDOCK/jog drive the real jetway; the crew use it before arrivals, at docking, before departure (pushback waits for the bridge) and for maintenance checks. Built in 7 parts, not published until he says.', '17-21 h (about 2.5 days)', ['none'], '', False),
    ('117', 'NEXT AFTER THE JETWAY WORK (115) - Your tablet, always with you (owner, 2026-11-18, photo docs/Pictures/Tablet/tablet-reference-photo.webp): (1) THE MODEL: a slim 11-inch tablet as in his photo - dark graphite aluminium body with rounded corners, an even black bezel all round, the front camera centred in the top bezel, a glass screen; modelled in the same detail as the smartphone (Art/Tablet/Tablet.blend, instanced + Nanite, own reference photos, no empty material slots); (2) PICKING IT UP: it is always with you - Tab takes it up and you actually HOLD IT IN YOUR HAND(S) (your character hands on it, seen in your view, its screen on, the UI live on it as on the jetway monitor); E (pad: X) brings its UI into FULL SCREEN; Esc / E back to holding it; Tab again puts it away; on a gamepad D-pad Down (free on foot: it only switches the high beams inside a truck); Tab opens Settings today - Settings moves to F10 (and stays on the pad Menu button); (3) THE SAME UI AND FEATURES AS THE PHONE (its apps, the phone frame in a tablet layout); (4) PLUS - ON THE TABLET AND BOTH PC MONITORS ONLY, NEVER THE PHONE: a button that opens the OVERVIEW (the screen with its many features shown before you enter the airport); the Overview and its features are reachable only from the two PCs (the operations monitor and your office computer) and the tablet - not from the phone; (5) BACK: the top-left logo of the Overview takes you back to the tablet (or the PC you came from) instead of the main title screen. (5b) LOOK (owner 2026-11-18): black, glossy only a little (about 10 gloss - satin), a small passenger aeroplane as the logo on the back, a stunning photoreal landscape as its wallpaper (first: Mulafossur at Gasadalur, CC0 photo by Eric Welch, Wikimedia Commons); (5c) A SETTINGS APP on the tablet AND the phone (and wallpaper on both PCs): WALLPAPER (built-in photoreal landscapes - the first his own drone photo of a Faroese sea stack, Art/Tablet/Wallpapers - + your own picture from your computer, with CROPPING when you choose it: drag and zoom the picture in a frame the shape of the screen, separate for home and lock screen), brightness and auto-brightness (darker at night), dark / light mode, text size, notifications per app (on/off, sound, banners), sounds and volumes (notification, ringtone, keyboard clicks, alarms), vibration (the gamepad rumbles), do not disturb (with a schedule, e.g. on shift), screen timeout and auto-lock, lock screen with an optional PIN, clock 12 / 24 h and date format, language, the battery (it drains with use and charges at a desk or in a car - low battery warning; can be switched off), about the device (model, storage, version); the same wallpaper choice on both PC desktops. (6) ONCE THE TABLET WORKS: the current ways into the Overview are switched off (not deleted) - New Game, Continue and Load Game never open the Overview (to-do 119 says where you start); the Overview only from the tablet and the in-game PCs. Wait for his photo-based model OK before building (blueprint/preview first).', '1.5-2.5 days (rough, to be split into parts)', ['none'], '', False),
    ('118', 'Multiplayer with Epic Online Services - free (owner, 2026-11-18): live play together in the 3D airport (seeing each other walk, drive, work the jetways) through Unreal own networking plus Epic Online Services (EOS, free for every developer): accounts / sign-in (Epic account or the player own Steam account), lobbies and sessions, matchmaking, friends and invites, voice chat, and the relay so one player hosts and the others join without a paid server; Supabase (his free account) kept for the slow shared things - saves online, the shared airline economy and market, leaderboards, trades, chat (the database, not Realtime messages: the free plan has 200 connections, 100 messages a second, 2 million a month). FIRST STEPS (his part): sign in at the Epic Games Developer Portal (dev.epicgames.com) with an Epic account, create an Organization and a Product for Airport Dynasty Pro, and share the product, sandbox and client IDs with me (never passwords). MY PART: switch on Unreal EOS plugin (Online Subsystem EOS / EOS Plus), sign-in, host/join a session in the Dev sandbox, replicate players (movement, animation), then the jetways, doors, vehicles and the HMI so every player sees the same; LOBBY + SERVER BROWSER (EOS Lobbies and Sessions): host your airport (name, player limit, public / friends / invite-only, password), a list of open airports (host, players 3/8, region, ping, lock), join from the list, an invite or a code, a lobby room before play (who is in, ready check, chat/voice, roles: gate agent, ground crew, tower); listen server first (one player hosts, free, EOS relay), a dedicated server later if needed; SAVED SERVERS THAT CONTINUE: the host airport save holds the whole shared world (economy, airlines, flights, staff, buildings, jetways, vehicles, game time, events) and every player progress keyed by their Epic account ID (role, rank, pay, skills, stats, place, items, looks); autosave every few minutes + on quit + when a player leaves, rolling backups (last 10 + one a day), a host list of saved servers with CONTINUE SERVER (re-registered with EOS under the same name and ID), returning players continue, new players start fresh under the host rules (public / friends / invite, password, permissions), optional copy of the server save in his free Supabase; HOST MIGRATION WITH TRUSTED CO-HOSTS (after the above, about 3-5 extra working days): the owner picks co-hosts in order; while hosting, the server streams an encrypted, signed snapshot of the airport to them every few seconds (changes only); if the host quits or drops, the first co-host online becomes the server from the latest snapshot, re-registers under the same server name/ID, everyone reconnects automatically (5-20 s, at most a few seconds lost); the owner returning can TAKE BACK HOSTING (state handed back, his save updated); if nobody is left, the last server saves and the save returns to the owner (Supabase / next meeting); the owner stays owner - co-hosts cannot delete, change ownership or the co-host list, nor use the copy as their own save; a weak or slow co-host is skipped for the next; test in pre-alpha in the Dev sandbox with a few testers (bugs expected - logged to ADP.log); Stage and Live sandboxes only for releases. Build in parts; nothing published until he says.', '4-8 days for a first playable version (rough, to be split into parts)', ['none'], '', False),
    ('119', 'Where you start and where you continue - never the Overview (owner, 2026-11-18): (1) NEW GAME (for now) starts you outside the main hall, 35 m from it, looking straight at the centre entrance door; (2) CONTINUE / LOAD puts you exactly where you were when you saved or when the game ended - position, facing, camera, and what you were doing: sitting on a chair (that chair), lying on a bed, in your car (that car and seat), holding the tablet, at a jetway HMI; (3) PUBLIC TRANSPORT: you cannot leave or quit while riding a bus, taxi, shuttle etc. - trying to brings you back to the game with a short note (Request to leave the bus first / the taxi ...); REQUEST TO LEAVE makes the vehicle stop at the next safe spot, the exit animation plays, it drives on without you, then you can quit (built into public transport when it comes with to-do 116); (4) HARD QUIT (task manager, closing the window, a crash, power cut): your place and state written every few seconds to a small file, so you come back at your last place; if you were on public transport, next to where you got on (not inside it); (5) THE WORLD MOVES ON while the game is off: real-world time of day and date, and on load the world is brought up to now behind the loading screen - flights landed, staff at work or at home by their own schedules, NPC lives, the economy and money; (6) MULTIPLAYER the same, per player by Epic ID in the host save, the world brought up to the real time when the host starts it again.', '2-3 days (rough, to be split into parts)', ['none'], '', False),
    ('114', 'DONE 2026-11-18 - Stair beside the arrivals escalators: steps and guard (owner, 2026-11-18, photos docs/Pictures/Escalator/guardrail-broken-photo.webp, wood-at-top-1..2-photo.webp, reference Art/Escalator/Reference/Owner-2026-11-18/3-steps-texture.jpg): the stair was oak ("the escalator stairs are wood, they should not be like that") - now the escalators own cleated metal steps with yellow edges (SM_StairStep, instanced + Nanite); its glass guard was kinked and broken at the top - now one glass ribbon under one smooth handrail with a round corner onto the landing, like the escalators guards. The escalator steps got dark grooves between the cleats as in his photo.', '2 h', ['none'], '', True),
    ('113', 'DONE 2026-11-18 - Wood floor too close in front of the escalators (owner, 2026-11-18, photos docs/Pictures/Escalator/wood-at-top-1..5-photo.webp): at the top of the arrivals escalators on Floor 2 the new wood landing floor runs onto the escalators - wood shows through the grooved floor plates and as a strip across the right escalator, so the top of the escalators reads as wood. The wood must stop short of each escalator: the escalator floor plates, combs and newel ends stay all metal, the wood meets them with a clean edge (Art/TerminalAnnex/make_arrivals_wing.py landing slabs, cut round the escalators top ends).', '1-2 h (rough, not measured)', ['none'], '', True),
    ('107', 'TOP PRIORITY (owner, 2026-11-18: today s items first, in reverse order 107 -> 100) - Hangar doors slide in the wrong order (owner, 2026-11-18, photo docs/Pictures/Hangar/door-leaf-order-photo.webp): the hangar doors are several telescoping leaves (right), but when they close the INNER leaf travels furthest. Reverse it: the inner leaf stays parked at the hangar s edge (by the jamb) and hardly moves, the OUTER leaf travels the furthest and the leaves between follow in turn, like a real telescopic hangar door; opening the same in reverse. All hangars, keep speeds, sounds and safety (never closing on anyone). GO given 2026-11-18', '1-2 h (rough, not measured)', ['none'], '', True),
    ('106', 'Dead end at the top of the baggage claim escalator - shrink the tax free (owner, 2026-11-18, photo docs/Pictures/Escalator/top-of-escalator-wall-photo.webp): riding the escalator up to Floor 2 you step off facing a gigantic blank wall - it is the tax free shop. Shrink the tax free hall in that direction so its end wall stands in the same plane as the wall between gate 11 and view bay 11 (plan x about -119.15; not up to the gate itself), until the escalator exit on Floor 2 is free (check whether a proper exit/landing exists there; make one if not). Everything the shrink touches moves with it or is reimagined: the tax free entrance and neon sign, its shelves, kiosks, pay points, seats and other furniture, the promenade, lights, the walk map/plan json. Then a screenshot of the fixed Floor 2 escalator exit and the smaller tax free for him. GO given 2026-11-18', '3-5 h (rough, not measured)', ['none'], '', True),
    ('105', 'Very big advert screen on the end wall over the escalator (owner, 2026-11-18, photo docs/Pictures/Escalator/end-wall-big-screen-photo.webp): the big plain end wall above the escalator (the baggage claim side) gets one very big monitor - large, but not filling the whole wall (a margin of wall all round), slim dark frame, running the game s advertisements like the other advert screens (same advert system, its own glow, readable from the escalator and the hall). Blueprint/position picture first for his OK. GO given 2026-11-18', '2-3 h (rough, not measured)', ['none'], '', True),
    ('104', 'Square patches on the glass (owner, 2026-11-18, photo docs/Pictures/Escalator/glass-squares-photo.webp): looking down at the baggage claim through the glass balustrade by the escalator, the glass shows faint square blocks. Find the cause (a tiled glass normal/texture, the reflection or the translucency shading in blocks, or frame generation) and make the glass clean and even. GO given 2026-11-18', '1-2 h (rough, not measured)', ['none'], '', True),
    ('103', 'Escalator steps far too reflective (owner, 2026-11-18, photo docs/Pictures/Escalator/steps-too-reflective-photo.webp): the moving steps (treads and risers) of the escalators mirror the surroundings strongly. The steps themselves only about 5 % reflective (low specular / high roughness, matte grooved metal); the other metal parts (balustrade trim, deck, comb plates) may stay reflective as they are. All escalators. GO given 2026-11-18', '0.5-1 h (rough, not measured)', ['none'], '', True),
    ('102', 'Baggage belt metal texture stretched (owner, 2026-11-18, photo docs/Pictures/BaggageBelt/metal-texture-stretched-photo.webp): on the belt housing (the hood walls and its side panels) the brushed-metal texture is smeared into long streaks in places - stretched UVs on scaled parts. Make it tile at real size and run seamlessly across every face (world-size UVs or re-unwrapped meshes, no streaks, no visible seams), all belts. GO given 2026-11-18', '1-2 h (rough, not measured)', ['none'], '', True),
    ('101', 'Baggage belt plates half hidden in the curve (owner, 2026-11-18, photo docs/Pictures/BaggageBelt/curve-plates-half-hidden-photo.webp): on the claim belt bend the slat plates the bags ride on show only half - something lies over their inner half (likely the belt body deck or a still plate layer sitting at or above the moving plates there, or the plates riding too low in the curve). Find what covers them; every plate fully visible all the way round, straights and curves. GO given 2026-11-18', '1-2 h (rough, not measured)', ['none'], '', True),
    ('100', 'Baggage belt hood down to the belt (owner, 2026-11-18, photo docs/Pictures/BaggageBelt/hood-flaps-gap-photo.webp): where the bags come up onto the claim belt, the hood housing walls and the rubber strip curtain stop short of the belt - a gap underneath. The housing walls go all the way down to the conveyor belt, and the rubber strips hang all the way down to it too (still swinging aside as each bag pushes through). GO given 2026-11-18', '1-2 h (rough, not measured)', ['none'], '', True),
    ('109', 'Stair beside the arrivals escalators - handrail end (owner, 2026-11-18, photo docs/Pictures/Escalator/stair-rail-end-photo.png): at the bottom the glass balustrade and its handrail stop abruptly, the glass standing on its own. The handrail (where people hold) carries on past the glass and bends down to the floor, ending in a small metal base plate on the floor - only slightly bigger than the rail - with screws, as if fixed to the floor; same at the top end if it ends the same way. Arrivals wing stair (Art/TerminalAnnex/make_arrivals_wing.py). INFO ONLY - build nothing until he says go', '0.5-1 h (rough, not measured)', ['none'], '', False),
    ('110', 'Escalator metal texture stretched (owner, 2026-11-18, photo docs/Pictures/Escalator/escalator-metal-stretched-photo.png): the brushed stainless on the escalators - the new inlet housings, the decking, the floor-plate ends, the cladding at the ends - is smeared into long streaks, very visible up close. Map every escalator face at real size (tileable, seamless, the brushing running one way), as done for the baggage belts (102): Art/Escalator/make_escalator.py Builder UVs, all escalators (hall + arrivals). INFO / to-do', '1-1.5 h (rough, not measured)', ['none'], '', True),
    ('111', 'DONE 2026-11-18 (built, not published) - Frame generation on/off without a restart (owner, 2026-11-18): every frame generator in Settings - our own frame generation and FSR / DLSS frame generation - switches on and off live while the game runs, fully working straight away (no restart, no half state: the swap chain / presentation set up again on the fly, the HUD and menus unaffected); the restart notice goes', '2-4 h (rough, not measured)', ['gfx'], '', True),
    ('112', 'Escalator ends - you can see through (owner, 2026-11-18, photo docs/Pictures/Escalator/escalator-end-gaps-photo.jpg): at the ends where the decking slopes down to the floor beside the inlet housings, the sloped end pieces have open slits/gaps between them and under them - you see through into the dark. Close them: the end ramps meet the skirt, the housings and each other flush, closed underneath and at the sides; all escalators (Art/Escalator/make_escalator.py). INFO ONLY - do not fix until he says go', '0.5-1 h (rough, not measured)', ['none'], '', True),
    ('94', 'FIRST - Floor 2 Food Walk opened up (owner, 2026-11-17, photo docs/Pictures/Terminal-Fixes/food-walk-wall; blueprint docs/Blueprints/FoodWalk-1-Plan.png and FoodWalk-2-Portal.png): the wall between the landing and the Food Walk (x 56, from the old tax free room corner to the gate walkway) goes, and the same at the other end (x 14, the link to the walkway); in each opening a very beautiful entrance portal, flush with the glass on one side and the wall end on the other (walnut pillars with a warm LED line, slatted wood header with a backlit FOOD WALK sign, a brass floor strip, planters, 7 m clear); no holes in what is left. CONFIRMED by him 2026-11-17 with changes: the sign lights up as neon with the same effects and settings as the tax free neon sign (pink, glow, flicker); wood as in the render; his chosen look: docs/Pictures/FoodWalk/REFERENCE-portal-neon.jpg and REFERENCE-from-landing.jpg (the whole place seen from the landing) (build it like that, neon effect included); real textures (the game ones first, CC0 web textures where none fits)', '3-4 h (rough, not measured)', ['none'], '', False),
    ('96', 'SECOND - the Food Walk eating area like the render (owner, 2026-11-17, his chosen look docs/Pictures/FoodWalk/REFERENCE-eating-area.jpg): the brown wood floor (the tax free / walkway wood, M_WoodFloor), the slatted wood ceiling with LED lines (the wood he liked - the hangar wood texture), round white tables with red chairs, brass pendant lamps over them, the eateries with lit menu boards and their own made-up names, plants in tall pots; real textures: the game ones first, CC0 textures from the web (Poly Haven / ambientCG) for the rest; instanced + Nanite; all chairs sittable (seat rules)', '4-6 h (rough, not measured)', ['none'], '', False),
    ('75', 'No stalls on first use (owner, 2026-11-17): a recorded shader (PSO) cache shipped with the game - recorded once from the full stress walk plus a weather run (day/night, rain, snow, fog, effects, people, pets) and prepared behind the loading screen on every start; plus a short warm-up pass behind the loading screen (night, rain, snow briefly, then back); the release script re-records it when materials change; the driver keeps its own rolling cache. Smoother (no 0.1-5.6 s first-time freezes, e.g. at nightfall), not a higher average fps', '3-4 h + test (rough, not measured)', ['none'], '', False),
    ('77', 'NVIDIA DLSS Super Resolution (free official plugin, commercial use allowed) as an upscaler choice in Settings next to TSR and FSR (Quality / Balanced / Performance), plus dynamic resolution with an fps target; tested together with our own frame generation (DLSS sharpens each frame, ours doubles them; never with FSR frame generation at the same time): menus, HUD and fast turns without smearing; measured in the shipping build in his window (estimate: Cinematic ~26 -> ~40 real fps with DLSS Quality). If it works: a short tip under both options in Graphics - DLSS and our frame generation work best together (DLSS raises the real fps, ours doubles it, smoother the higher the real fps) - a suggestion only, never switched on by itself; shown on RTX cards, other cards get the FSR/TSR tip', '4-6 h (rough, not measured)', ['none'], '', False),
    ('76', 'More fps at Cinematic (owner, 2026-11-17): relieve the graphics memory (7.7 of 8 GB at 3840x1080 - what fills it, cheaper pools without visible loss), check the 35 lights that redraw moving shadows every frame, finer Lumen reflection / TSR tuning - each measured in the shipping build in his window, never visibly worse', '3-5 h (rough, not measured)', ['none'], '', False),
    ('74', 'Aircraft traffic stress test (owner, 2026-11-17): a test-only switch (-TrafficTest=N) fills all 16 gate stands and the cargo and hangar stands with N aircraft and slides test aircraft along the taxiway and runway centre lines and a straight approach line (test movement only - the aircraft are static models, no flight logic or animation yet; real air traffic is its own feature, blueprint first; normal game unchanged); stand test with 0/10/20/40 aircraft, day and night, shipping build in his window; then split the cost per aircraft (lights, heat haze, moving parts, windows) and fix what costs most without visible loss - polygons stay as they are (Nanite handles them) [switch written 2026-11-17 - waiting for compile + the stand tests]', '3-4 h + fixes (rough, not measured)', ['none'], '', False),
    ('80', 'Flicker when scrolling with frame generation on (owner, 2026-11-17): scrolling the Settings lists and other menus, and the screens/windows inside the terminal, flicker - likely our own frame generation guessing the in-between frame for UI and screens that move without motion vectors; find the cause (test with it off/on), then fix it - e.g. draw the menus after the generated frame, give moving screens and scroll areas proper motion vectors or keep them out of the generation - so frame generation stays on without the flicker [written 2026-11-17: no in-between pictures while menus are up, real frame where the guess does not fit - waiting for compile + his look]', '3-5 h (rough, not measured)', ['gfx'], '', False),
    ('82', 'Clouds: much faster, and a quality slider (owner, 2026-11-17: Clear shows broken clouds and the fps drops below 20; without clouds 50-60). Found by reading the code, to be confirmed with a GPU profile: 1) Clear is not clear - the cloud material treats coverage as plus/minus round its own default, so our 0.02 still gives broken cover, and the clouds are drawn even when none should show; 2) the sky light recaptures the whole sky with its clouds again every frame (real-time capture); 3) the engine sample cloud material is heavy (layers, storm and lightning layers, 3D noise) traced through a 10 km thick layer (5-15 km) at 3840x1080. Fix without losing the look: Clear = clouds off (none drawn), a lighter own cloud material, fewer samples where unseen (the cloud layer height and thickness NOT changed by hand - owner, 2026-11-17: the real-world weather data decides cloud height, type, rain, snow, wind, thunder, updrafts, for the whole world), the sky light recapture slowed (it changes slowly); then Settings > Graphics > Effects: Clouds on/off + quality slider. Stress test before/after', '4-6 h (rough, not measured)', ['gfx'], '', False),
    ('83', 'Falling snow and rain, lightning and thunder (owner, 2026-11-17: Snow chosen in the weather menu, no snow falls): flakes and drops round the camera, how hard by the weather (light / heavy, storm wind), cheap GPU particles; and in storms lightning bolts with thunder (owner, 2026-11-17): a real-looking bolt that lights up the clouds round it, the ground, the apron and the terminal for its flash (the sky light catches the flash at once, then back to slow updates), thunder after it by the distance (light first, sound later; Freesound CC0, heard from where you stand), now and then, more in heavy storms; part of the weather build (with puddles, wet ground, snow on the ground)', '3-4 h (rough, not measured)', ['gfx'], '', False),
    ('84', 'Jetway tunnel sections flush, not clipping (owner, 2026-11-17, do not build until he says): the three telescoping tunnel sections sit 25-30 percent into each other; each must slide flush along the outside of the next smaller one - keep the order (widest stays widest, then second, then the smallest), widen the middle one so it rides flush over the smallest, then the widest even wider so it rides flush over the middle one (edit the geometry, not a blunt scale, so walls, floor, roof and window frames keep their thickness). Everything that touches them follows exactly: the rotunda end meets the widened section at the same point it does now; the cab end and its rubber seal to the aircraft only if needed; the rubber joint seals between the sections widened to meet the wider parts; the legs / lift columns checked against the widest part (no clipping); every object on top or on the sides (lights, pipes, hoses, boxes, the beacon) stays in its exact spot on its section. Service door: a solid wall round it where the X-frame window is now (part of the window goes), the door opens, nothing in the way, through it to the small outside stair, and both sides reachable. Service door look (inspiration, photo docs/Pictures/Jetway-Fixes/ref-service-door - a bought model, reference only): a solid light grey door panel in a wall section with a framed window in its upper half, a vertical pull bar, hinges on one side, a small wall lamp over it, opening onto a small landing with tubular guardrails and the stair down; web reference photos of real jetway service doors and their details first (reference-photos rule), his own jetway pieces only (jetway rules: REST POINT, front door, hoses in pipes); Blender pre-export checks (empty slots, z-fight), then the animation checked for clipping at every extension. When done, send him (owner, 2026-11-17): in-game stills of the jetway from inside, straight down from above, the side, the front, the back, and looking down along it; the same angles in wireframe; a grey-box (clay) render of the same angles; ONE video from one angle while the jetway moves; the finished jetway high-detail 4K PNGs e-mailed to him (official.pixelnorma@gmail.com) (game window only; owner: only one angle) - all of it sent only once the sections align and nothing clips; and a fixed camera inside (no walking) watching the sections move, checked for clipping. More fixes from his photos come first - wait for them', '8-10 h (rough, not measured)', ['none'], '', False),
    ('84a', 'Jetway fixes from his photos (owner, 2026-11-17, do not build until he says; photos in docs/Pictures/Jetway-Fixes): 1) the small light (beacon) on top of the wheel bogie, just above the wheels: its base is too wide and sticks out past the surface it stands on - make the base narrower so it sits fully on that surface (close-up fix-01b: the bolted base plate overhangs the box edge at the front) - shrink only the base plate (and its bolts) so it sits inside the box top with a small margin, the lamp and pole keep their size; if the pole is too near the edge for that, move the whole light a little inward instead; the same for the beacon on every jetway; 2) the yellow hoses: replaced by fix 13 (owner, same day); 3) inside the cab (the room with the aircraft door and the control desk): the walls with the darker brushed-metal texture (the pillar by the door, the panels behind the desk) get the lighter grey wall / facade texture instead, all of that surface; and the other spots in the jetway with the same dark metal on walls - find them and give them the light one too (floors, ceiling slats, the door and the desk stay as they are); 4) the rotunda inside: the curved walls look plain white, as if the grey metal plate texture is missing there - go over every surface of the jetway (rotunda, tunnel sections, cab, ceilings) and put the grey metal plate texture wherever it is missing, and make it look right: not stretched on the curves, panel seams lined up with the real panel joints, the same scale as the other walls (pictures of each spot before and after); 5) the ceiling with the dome camera (photo fix-05) - waiting for him to say what is wrong there; 6) the rotunda floor: remove the blue wheelchair sign; and the yellow-black hazard stripe lying across the rotunda floor (it shows a graphics problem - photo fix-06b): remove that section and fill it level with the same carpet it sits on, evenly flat, no step, no seam, no flicker (the other stripes and the tactile plates stay); 7) the dark brushed-metal corner post between two inside wall panels (photo fix-07) gets the light grey wall texture too. All the light grey wall texture changes (fixes 3, 4, 7) are for the INSIDE of the jetway only - the outside keeps its own look; 8) the wheel centre caps (hub domes) on the jetway wheels (photo fix-01): make them smooth and round, no visible facets (more segments or smooth shading), on every wheel of every jetway; 9) the X-frame window bars stay as they are, but their texture (the smooth light painted metal, photo fix-09) goes on the OUTSIDE walls of the three sliding tunnel sections only - not the rotunda, not the rubber seals / joint seals, not the cab, not the legs (for now: start with the tunnel outer walls); no stretching or bad tiling, seams lined up; 10) the gaps at the joints inside (photos fix-07 and fix-10): at the opening where one part meets the next (rotunda to tunnel, and between the sliding sections) you can see into the gap on the left side, the right side and along the top under the roof - seal them off with an inside trim frame in the same light grey wall texture, fitting perfectly smooth and flush with the inside wall pieces and the roof / ceiling pieces (no step, no seam, no flicker), fixed to the part it belongs to so it slides with it and never blocks or clips the slide at any extension; 11) the rubber seal (canopy) at the cab front (reference photo fix-11): seen straight on it keeps its outer size, still covering the whole frame of the cab, but its two side bands reach about half as far in towards the doorway (cut / shortened to half their inner width), so the opening is wider - proportions as in photo fix-11b (straight on: slim padded side bands, about a tenth of the cab width each, a deeper padded top band, the bands following the cab frame and meeting it perfectly); the top band stays as it is; the folds, lining and its open / close movement adjusted to match; 12) the rubber seal look (reference photo fix-12): dark grey coated fabric in soft rounded pleats, thin light ribs (stiffeners) along every fold, the padded bumper edge round the outside, slightly worn and dirty - a new texture (colour, normal, roughness) laid along the folds so it follows the geometry perfectly, no stretching; the same look on the bellows / joint seals between the rotunda and the tunnel and between the tunnel and the cab, following their folds as they stretch; 13) the two yellow air hoses (reference photos fix-13-hoses-ref-1/2): BOTH come out of the PCA unit under the jetway (84b); one hangs in a loose loop, not connected (stowed, as in the photos); the other runs up and is connected into the metal tube; they move along with the jetway and are a little alive: a gentle sway and settle when the jetway moves, never floppy like cloth; the sway can be switched off in Settings > Graphics > Animations (to-do 98)', 'with 84', ['none'], '', False),
    ('84b', 'Jetway underside details, photoreal (owner, 2026-11-17, reference photo docs/Pictures/Jetway-Fixes/ref-underside-details; do not build until he says): under the rotunda the radial steel beams to a ring, the round drum edge and its flange; under the corridor and the three tunnel sections the steel floor frame (stringers and cross-members), the perforated galvanised cable trays with the black cables in loose bundles and loops, conduits, junction boxes, brackets and bolts, drip edges; not the big boxes or the wheels (those stay). Built to extend: each section carries its own frame and tray (moves with it), the cables between sections run in a loop / cable carrier that pays out and folds back as the tunnel telescopes, the rotunda underside static, nothing clipping at any extension. More reference photos from the web for what this photo does not show (cable runs at the joints, the tunnel underside close up) first. Also the big grey box hung under the tunnel by the drive unit (photo ref-pca-unit-under-tunnel): the pre-conditioned air unit (PCA) - a grey cabinet with louvres, service panels, labels, hung on brackets under the section it belongs to (moves with it), its yellow air hose fed from it; real web photos of PCA units under jetways first, built photoreal - look from his references ref-air-unit-1/2: a dark grey box on two hanger frames under the tunnel, a big louvred grille face in three panels, a filter grille on its end, the yellow hose out of its underside; bolts, hinges, screws, gussets and brackets modelled wherever the references show them. And the lower part of the three sliding tunnel sections outside (photo ref-sill-beam-under-windows): the sill the windows rest on, modelled - a stepped steel sill beam (a glazing bead and drip ledge under the glass, a recessed channel, then the deep side beam with its lower lip), the ends of the floor frame showing below, in the jetway own colours, photoreal; on all three sections, moving with each', '6-8 h (rough, not measured)', ['none'], '', False),
    ('84c', 'Jetway cab console like the real one (owner, 2026-11-17, photos docs/Pictures/Jetway-Fixes/ref-console-1 and -2-labelled; more photos coming): a light grey steel cabinet on its plinth, a sloped brushed stainless top plate with screws at its corners, the operator panel display under a hinged stainless sun hood, two columns of round push buttons (controls) and a key switch, the black joystick with its rubber gaiter, the red mushroom emergency stop on its yellow ring, the green / red / amber lamps and the red button with their labels, the louvred filter vents on the cabinet front, a warning label, the door to the cabinet; textures taken from the photos (photoreal), modelled in 3D; it stays where the console is now (E at it still opens Dock / Undock)', '3-4 h (rough, not measured)', ['none'], '', False),
    ('84d', 'Rotunda support column like the reference (owner, 2026-11-17, photos docs/Pictures/Jetway-Fixes/ref-rotunda-column-1/2 - a bought model shown for reference only: we build our own, never buy): the column holding up the rotunda replaced by this design - a round steel column in stepped sections with collar rings, a base flange with triangular gussets and anchor bolts on a dark concrete foundation pad, at the top a sloped cantilever bracket into the rotunda floor frame, a small electrical box on the pad; photoreal, the jetway colours; ask him whether one column (as in the photo) or keep two', '2-3 h (rough, not measured)', ['none'], '', False),
    ('85', 'Jetways at the new gates (owner, 2026-11-17: some gates have none - photo new-gate-without-jetway): part 4 of the 16-gate build, not done before 0.17.21 - west gates 9 and 11 one jetway each (like gates 1 and 5); east gate 13 (A380) three, gates 14-16 (B777) two each = 11 new jetways, his own jetway pieces only (no stand-in copies), each lined up with the front door(s) of its aircraft type, REST POINT half way back, hoses in pipes, the stand markings and stop bars matching; with the fixes of 84 / 84a in them from the start; walk-out gates 10 and 12 have none by design - ask him if he wants jetways there too', '6-8 h (rough, not measured)', ['none'], '', True),
    ('85b', 'Doorways in the glass for the wide-body bridges (found 2026-11-17 while building 85): the new east gate rooms are copies of the gate-4 pair, whose one jetway doorway sits west of the stand, so the bridges of gates 13-16 (built on the east side, at the aircraft left doors, as the blueprint) meet plain glass - cut a doorway with its frame and door in the glass at each of the 9 bridges (gate room / view bay), a gate desk route to each; until then those bridges work and dock but you cannot walk into them from the gate', '3-4 h (rough, not measured)', ['none'], '85', False),
    ('86', 'Tax free: two glass shop units face the wrong way (owner, 2026-11-17: there are two of these; photo docs/Pictures/TaxFree-Fixes/fix-01): both glass boxes with shelves near the Aurora Plaza, by the window side - its closed glass side faces the promenade, so you cannot walk in; the way in is only from behind, through a very narrow gap by the windows. Turn both round (mirror them) so their open side faces the promenade and it is easy to walk in; keep the walkway behind it wide enough or close it off; then check every other tax free unit the same way (each open to the promenade, room to walk in)', '1-2 h (rough, not measured)', ['none'], '', False),
    ('87', 'Tax free: the long tasting bar by the windows near gate 11 becomes a snack bar (owner, 2026-11-17, photo docs/Pictures/TaxFree-Fixes/fix-02): you walk up and buy hot dogs, burgers, wraps and similar food - a serving counter with a food display / warmer, a lit menu board on the back wall instead of the plain lit panel, a grill / hot-dog roller and fryer behind, napkins and sauces, made-up names only (no real brands); a few bar chairs along its side; the stools now standing in front of the counter moved so nothing stands in front of where you order (exact spot to be agreed); E / X to buy later with the vending / talk systems; reference photos of airport snack bars first, blueprint for his OK before building', '3-4 h (rough, not measured)', ['none'], '', False),
    ('88', 'Sitting (owner, 2026-11-17, photo docs/Pictures/Seat-Fixes/fix-01, a tax free bar stool): 1) the You seem to be stuck panel must never show while you sit on a chair you chose (E to sit, E to stand works) - only when truly stuck; 2) the sitting pose looks wrong: legs crossing oddly, arms crossed in front of the body - make it a natural sitting pose for every kind of seat: on a chair or bench feet flat on the floor, knees apart a little, hands resting on the thighs or the armrests; on a high bar stool feet on its foot ring or rung, hands on the knees or the counter; the body fitted to each seat height (no floating, no sinking in), the same for every chair in the game, new ones too (seat rules); hand rules: natural limits, fingers relaxed', '2-3 h (rough, not measured)', ['none'], '', False),
    ('91', 'Floor 2 gate rooms and view bays open to the walkway (owner, 2026-11-17, photo docs/Pictures/Terminal-Fixes/gate-room-back-wall): every gate room / view bay on Floor 2 (old and new, gates 1-16) has a wall on the walkway side with only a door-like opening in it (the wall behind you when you sit looking out of the window). Delete that back wall so the room is open to the walkway and easy to walk into; keep the sign (hung from the ceiling or the beam over the opening); no holes in what is left: the side walls, ceiling, floor edge and the walkway wall ends finished clean, flush, no gaps, no flicker, the ceilings meeting edge to edge; the seats, lamps, CCTV spots and the walkway handrails / moving walkways checked; pictures of each gate after', '2-3 h (rough, not measured)', ['none'], '', False),
    ('92', 'Tax free promenade end at the entrance (owner, 2026-11-17, photo docs/Pictures/TaxFree-Fixes/fix-03): where the marble promenade ends at the doors there is a sliver of wood floor between its end and the white line that runs across the entrance - extend the promenade (and its pink neon edges) so its end sits flush against that white line all the way across, no gap, no overlap, no flicker; the same at the other door (way out)', '1 h (rough, not measured)', ['none'], '', False),
    ('93', 'The dark far end of the gate walkway (owner, 2026-11-17, photo docs/Pictures/Terminal-Fixes/walkway-dark-far-end): cause found - the frame-saving lamp limit from 2026-11-16 (UI/ADPHUD.cpp: every lamp light switched off beyond 150 m on foot, 600 m flying); with 16 gates the walkway is about 800 m long, so its far part has no lamp light and the dark patch moves away as you walk. Owner: a frame-saving limit must not be on by default. Fix: a setting in Settings > Graphics > Effects (Lamp light distance: a slider, OFF = no limit) with OFF as the default, the old limit only when the player picks it; measure the frame cost of OFF at night (stand + walk) and report it; also check that Lumen lights the far walkway (Lumen scene distance) once the lamps reach', '1-2 h (rough, not measured)', ['gfx'], '', False),
    ('95', 'The blank wall near the baggage claim (owner, 2026-11-17, photo docs/Pictures/Terminal-Fixes/wall-near-baggage-claim): he says it is all the way over to the left on the Food Walk plan (Floor 2, past the Link, over the baggage claim wing below) - find that exact wall; and the same story on the right (east) end: wherever the baggage claim area underneath has no way up to Floor 2 / no way through, the same solution (open it up so people can walk through); look at both ends and report what is behind each wall first. If the claim hall is behind it: open that wall so people walk straight over (a wide opening), every edge finished, no holes or gaps left; if not, report what is behind it. The room under the tax free has no use yet - ask him what it should become', '1-2 h (rough, not measured)', ['none'], '', False),
    ('97', 'Stress test of the changed areas (owner, 2026-11-17): once the terminal items (94, 96, 91, 89, 92, 95, 86, 87, 90) and the missing jetways (85) are built, a stress walk through exactly the areas that changed (Food Walk and its portals, the eating area, the gate rooms, the tax free, the new gates with their jetways, the baggage claim wall), shipping build in his window, frame generation on, day and night; compare with the last test, fix what got slower', '1-2 h (rough, not measured)', ['rel'], '85', True),
    ('98', 'Settings > Graphics > Animations (owner, 2026-11-17): a new category with on/off switches for the small extra movements - the jetway yellow hoses swaying as the jetway moves; the plastic strip curtain where the bags come out onto the baggage belts (the strips swing as each bag pushes through - to be built); later ones join it. The main animations (the jetway moving, doors, belts running) can never be switched off; saved in ADP.Graphics like the Effects', '2-3 h (rough, not measured)', ['gfx'], '', True),
    ('99', 'Website news: the jetways built like the real thing (wording: built, never rebuilt) (owner, 2026-11-17): a post on HIS WordPress / Elementor site on the Synology - NEVER the old GitHub website (its life there is over, never touched again); only when he says go and how, and only once the jetway work (84, 84a, 84b, 85) is in a published build. Draft text in docs/Website-Jetway-News.md for his OK first', '0.5 h (rough, not measured)', ['none'], '84', False),
