    ('72', 'Baggage claim (arrivals wing) in the museum hall’s place, x -80..0 flush with the gates: 5 belts with feed shafts, crew input conveyors in the sorting hall, escalators/stair/lift down from the gates walkway, customs, arrivals hall, outside - docs/Baggage-Claim-Concept.md. Game: belts run only when started (crew button at the input, passengers’ start/stop button on the skirt; outline glow, button pressed in and out), bags fixed to the belt, only crew put on / take off at the input, only passengers take off in the hall, rubber flaps swing aside like cloth as bags pass and settle back, escalators animated; sounds: start one-shot with its fade-out, run loop seamless until stopped then fades out ~1.5 s; every landing: ground crew empty the hold, carry the bags to that flight’s belt input and start it', '3 days', ['none'], '', False),
    ('73a', 'Vending machines, part 1 (owner, 2026-11-17): blueprint of FIVE different kinds (e.g. cold drinks, snacks, hot coffee, fresh food / sandwiches, travel essentials), drawn from web reference photos with every real brand removed - our own made-up graphics only; all lit up (glowing fronts, lit shelves); renders of each for his OK before modelling', '3-4 h (rough, not measured)', ['none'], '', False),
    ('73b', 'Vending machines, part 2: the five modelled and placed in the terminal, illuminated; walk up and look through the glass at what is inside', '4-6 h (rough, not measured)', ['none'], '73a', False),
    ('73c', 'Vending machines, part 3, buying: E / X at the keypad opens a big, very beautiful pop-up UI (the machine keypad is too small to hit) where you cycle the numbers and pick the product; pay; the spiral / arm turns and the item drops down; you take it out of the flap below. Exact flow to be agreed with him first', '4-6 h (rough, not measured)', ['none'], '73b', False),
    ('67', 'PERF/LOOK, needs your OK: 12 Nanite meshes with see-through glass (bag-drop screens, stair balustrades, the gate walkway’s glass roof, the car rental) drawn the ordinary way so their glass shows as designed (Nanite can’t draw see-through parts)', '30 min', ['none'], '', False),
    ('130', 'LATER (owner, 2026-11-19) - Crash sounds when driving: real-world recordings (Freesound CC0, our sound rule), picked to sound very good and believable, played from where the crash is (sound placement rule). Separate sets for BIG vehicles (fire trucks, buses, tugs, fuel trucks and other airport vehicles) and SMALL ones (cars, personal vehicles, small airport carts). Kinds: (1) hitting something and still standing on your wheels; (2) tipping over / rolling; (3) a high-speed crash; (4) a low-speed crash (bump, scrape). Strength chosen by the impact speed and the vehicle mass, with glass, metal and debris layers so no two crashes sound the same.', '3-4 h', ['none'], '', False),
    ('131', 'LATER (owner, 2026-11-19; with 130 crash sounds) - Feel the weight of every vehicle: each vehicle gets its own real weight data (kerb weight, centre of mass, engine power, brakes, grip) set by its size and kind - a small car, a personal SUV, a van, a tug, a bus, a fuel truck, the 28 t fire truck (which already drives on its own real weight). The weight directly changes how it drives: how fast it picks up speed, how long it takes to stop, body roll and lean in corners, nose dip under braking and squat when accelerating, the suspension settling over bumps, sliding when pushed too hard, and how much it shoves or is shoved in a crash (a heavy truck barely notices a small car). Reference for the feel: the weighty, physical driving of big open-world games such as Grand Theft Auto - our own handling, tuned per vehicle. Gamepad rumble and camera shake scaled by weight too.', '4-6 h', ['none'], '', False),
    ('132', 'LATER - NOT YET, start only when the owner says (owner, 2026-11-19) - Traffic and vehicles, with 130 crash sounds and 131 vehicle weight built in the same job: vehicle models (several cars, a bus, a taxi) 6-10 h; car traffic on the road network (lanes, junctions, giving way, zebra crossings, parking) 10-14 h; the town-airport bus line (timetable, stops; shared with 116 part 5) 4-6 h; taxis (forecourt rank, drop-off and pick-up tied to passengers) 5-7 h; airport vehicles on the apron (tugs, fuel, catering, apron buses serving the stands) 12-18 h. About 37-55 working hours, plus 130 and 131.', '37-55 h', ['none'], '', False),
    ('133', 'LATER - PREVIEWS FIRST (owner, 2026-11-19) - Jetway wheels steer: when the jetway moves sideways (left/right), the wheel bogie - the mount the wheels are fixed on, not only the wheels - turns towards the way it is going, as real jetways do, and the wheels turn with it. Gradual: the bogie starts turning well ahead of the sideways move, so it looks believable, and turns back as the move ends. If the 3D model has no turning mount that looks like the real mechanism (a steering ring / turntable over the bogie), model it in. BEFORE BUILDING: a blueprint and an in-game screenshot for his OK. If the bogie has to be built in 3D: research real close-up photos of a jetway wheel bogie and model it with full detail (no saving on detail, it should look very good); be absolutely sure which parts actually turn the bogie (the slewing ring / king pin, the steering motor or cylinder, the linkage) and animate exactly those.', '5-7 h', ['none'], '', False),
    ('134', 'LATER - after 116 staff daily life and 69 people talk (owner, 2026-11-19; the idea as in the submarine game UBOAT, built our own way) - Take control of any crew member. You play your own avatar, but at any time you can take over any of YOUR crew/staff (not passengers or other NPCs). (1) Walk up to a crew member: crew members (only they) get the outline; E / X opens their menu - talk and questions as with passengers, mostly about their job (praise, comments, job questions), plus CONTROL THIS PERSON with a small line under it: taking control means you must follow and complete their duties; failing them hurts the work they are doing and, at worst, what depends on it. (2) You then ARE that person: their body, their place in the rota, their duties at the times they are due (the duty list from 116 part 7), the HUD showing their next task; switch back to your own avatar any time (your avatar waits where you left it, or walks on with simple AI). (3) Mid-dialogue: if they were talking to someone, you pick up exactly where the AI left the conversation, as if you had started it - the game keeps the dialogue state of every NPC (who, which line, which options are open) so control hands over cleanly. (4) Conduct: what you do as them counts ONLY on their record (good or bad, stored in their history) - never on the ranking of your own avatar or conduct; as them it is easier to earn very bad conduct (the game advises against it, but lets you); later you can call them to your office for a talk about it. (5) Their AI takes over again when you leave them, carrying on the duty from where you left it.', '20-30 h', ['none'], '', False),
    ('135', 'LATER (owner, 2026-11-19) - Key bindings by activity: only the keys of what you are doing right now are active - on foot, holding the phone or tablet, at a PC, at the jetway console, driving, in a dialogue, and so on. Picking up a device, sitting at a PC or getting into a vehicle switches to that set (more keys free for it, the same key may mean something else there); putting it down / getting out switches back to what your avatar is doing. Settings > Controls lists the bindings in those categories, each rebindable; the same key may be used in two categories (only one is active at a time). Built on Unreal Enhanced Input mapping contexts.', '6-8 h', ['none'], '', False),
    ('136', 'ASKED (owner, 2026-11-19) - Ray tracing as an on/off switch: not in the game yet (r.RayTracing=False since the start; Lumen traces in software). Turn on ray-tracing support for the project once (bigger shaders, the shader cache recorded again), then Settings > Graphics: Ray-traced lighting and reflections, OFF by default, on/off after a restart. Sharper reflections (glass, escalator metal, puddles, aircraft), exact light indoors; costs frame rate and about 1-2 GB more VRAM (his card runs near 7.8/8 GB at 32:9).', '3-4 h', ['none'], '', False),
    ('137', 'LATER (owner, 2026-11-19) - Sitting IK that knows the seat: measured each time - seat height, a backrest behind, a footrest below, the seat in front - so bar stools (perched, feet on the footrest ring), normal chairs and benches, aircraft cabin seats (reclined, short legroom, armrests) and bus seats each get the right pose, with the IK step (seat surface, hands on thighs, crossed thighs kept apart, thighs on the cushion) on all of them - for the passengers, the crew and your own avatar.', '4-6 h', ['none'], '', False),
    ('138', 'LATER (owner, 2026-11-19) - Hand IK for anything held (phone, tablet, cup, bag handle, tools, the hose): the palm and each finger joint placed on the real surface of the object (measured on its model each time), flush with no gap and never through it; the thumb on the side button or screen when it presses, the wrist and arm following naturally - for your avatar, the passengers and the crew. Also solves the phone hand (now behind the -PhoneHand switch).', '5-7 h', ['none'], '', False),
    ('139', 'CHECK (owner, 2026-11-19) - The in-game updater offers the same update again: he updated twice from inside the game, and the next start said the same update was there and downloaded it again. After a successful patch the game must remember its new version (the installed version file / the version compiled into the exe after the patch) and not offer it again; check what the update zip replaces, what version the patched game reports, and the updater log.', '1-2 h', ['none'], '', False),
    ('140', 'APPROVED (owner, 2026-11-19) - People detail by floor and sight (with the passengers-in-coaches detail work): same floor as you = always the best detail; the other floor and out of sight = simplified and nearly free (lowest LOD, hair cards, animation slowed or paused); the other floor but in view (balcony edge, Grand Hall opening, foot of the escalator) = better detail by distance with the smooth dithered fade, so it never looks wrong. Unreal Significance Manager + animation budget; proven with the stress walk (fps, VRAM, before and after).', '4-6 h', ['none'], '', False),
    ('141', "DECISION (owner, 2026-11-19, later) - The forecourt aspens through the seasons: their leaf material has season settings (Season Color, Season Strength): green in summer, golden-yellow in September-October like Icelandic aspens, bare in winter (the leaf layer switched off), from the game's calendar. He decides when.", '2-3 h', ['none'], '', False),
    ('142', "TO DO (owner, 2026-11-19) - Record the shader cache again (Tools/make_pso_cache.sh, SM6 keys) with the aspens in: Nanite assemblies (r.Nanite.AllowAssemblies=1) add new shaders, so the first look at the trees doesn't stutter - before the next release.", '1-2 h', ['none'], '', True),
    ('143', 'LATER (owner, 2026-11-19: not now) - Wind sway for the aspens (their skeletal wind); measured first.', '1-2 h', ['none'], '', False),
    ('144', "Airport coach, its Unreal part (done 2026-11-19): walk in through the doors (collision by shape on every part, the capsule hip-wide inside), every passenger seat sittable with the sitting IK (not the driver's), front + middle doors and the 6 luggage bays open with E/X (the coach kneels with the air hiss), lamps (low/high beams with 2 real lights for the nearest coach only, turn signals, tail, brake, reverse), mirrors that reflect (nearest coach within 25 m), drive hum muffled inside, the airport's name on the rear plate at run time. Measured: real beams 0.8 ms, mirrors 1.6 ms. Carrying your own luggage into a bay, the AI driver and AI passengers' luggage come with the traffic job (132).", 'done', ['none'], '', True),
    ('145', 'DECISION (owner, 2026-11-19: leave it for now) - The coaches look too dark in the game (navy near black, the teal sweep glowing green): material tuning, when he says.', '1-2 h', ['none'], '', False),
    ('146', "TO DO (owner, 2026-11-19) - The coaches' own stress test (not the terminal walk): from the nearer car park behind the terminal over the zebra crossing, looking at the coaches, up close, into one and through it (no clipping), then the coaches seen from inside the main hall; his usual report (fps + VRAM, Full HD + 4K + his screen, day + night, frame gen on). Needs 144's walking inside.", '2-3 h', ['none'], '', False),
    ('148', "DECIDED (owner, 2026-11-20; after 149) - Wheels always on the road: the parked coaches go through the ground (each set at its stand middle's height only). Fix as a rule for the WHOLE road network, now and every future road: every vehicle placed or parked on a road measures the road under each wheel and sits on it, following the road's slope slightly (pitch and roll as the surface is - a degree or so, as a real bus on a cambered road), every wheel touching, none sunk or floating. Check all 7 coaches at wheel height, and every other vehicle on the roads.", '2-3 h', ['none'], '', False),
    ('151', "TO DO - DO NOT BUILD YET (owner, 2026-11-20) - The title screen's Stress walk button gets the full walk as a choice: the terminal walk, the coaches' walk, or both in one go (the terminal, then the coaches by day and night - already in the game as -UIFullWalk / Tools/perf_walk.sh both).", '0.5-1 h', ['none'], '', False),
    ('152', "DONE 2026-11-20 (owner) - The frame savers in Settings > Graphics > Effects (Upscaler at Quality, Faster clouds, Faster lighting): their text says they give more frames per second and are highly recommended if your system is struggling; all three ON by default to save frames (each still switched off live, no restart).", 'done', ['gfx'], '', True),
    ('153', "TODAY'S TOP 6 No. 6 (owner, 2026-11-20) - The Overview reworked for Unreal: nothing in the Overview may open the Babylon 3D; every place in it works in Unreal; the PC and Android versions still talk to each other so flights (and the rest) stay in sync. AFTER 154 (owner, 2026-11-20): the Overview UI itself remade in Unreal with Unreal graphics, wired to the new AI logic - the AI airlines and the NPCs and everything else, the economy, every single part the current Overview has, all in the new version and fully working. ITS LOOK: very nice, dynamic and responsive (animated, reacting to every action, fitting every screen size). ITS DESIGN AND LAYOUT: previews shown to the owner first, and nothing more of that part is built until he approves them.", 'days (plan first)', ['none'], '', False),
    ('155', "WITH 154 + 153 (owner, 2026-11-20) - One simulation, seen everywhere. (1) The 3D world shows what the AI logic and the Overview's stats say: passengers come by AI coaches (AI drivers, MetaHuman passengers inside) and cars from the town and other areas, get out, check in, go through security, up to Floor 2, walk about, sit, lean on walls, shop, board at their gates and sit in the cabins; some break the rules - security, if needed the airport police, the stun gun as planned (69a-i); the generated voices, prompts and text with it - everything already planned for people, staff, traffic and conduct. (2) Live tracking on every Overview: the Android tablet app, the in-game tablet and the in-game PCs show a map with the coaches on their routes from each area to the airport and how many people sit in them, every person in the terminal as a blue dot, a red dot where there is bad conduct and security or police have been called; passengers seen going to the gates and into the aircraft, and once the cabins are built, where they sit and walk inside. (3) What the 3D world does not have yet (cabins, more routes): the groundwork laid now in the one shared data model (a place for it, marked not finished), filled in as each part is built. Plan with 154.", 'many days (with 154)', ['people'], '154, 153', False),
    ('156', "WITH 154/153 (owner, 2026-11-20) - NO web functions from Babylon at all in the Unreal game: everything that today runs as web pages inside the game (the title screen, the new-game steps and creator overlays, the Overview, menus, Settings pages) remade natively in Unreal from scratch. The title screen rewritten in Unreal keeping its graphics and animations if they can be remade; the AIRPORT DYNASTY PRO neon sign animation exactly as it looks today (remade in Unreal, or captured from the current one as a film played by Unreal if that is the only way to keep it exact). Previews for the owner as with the Overview.", 'many days (with 154)', ['none'], '154, 153', False),
    ('157', "WITH 156 (owner, 2026-11-20) - Title music in Unreal: the title screen's soundtrack imported; more soundtracks can be added later (a folder / list - drop a track in), played in random order; music ONLY on the title screen and its sub-screens (new game, character creator, pets, spouse, difficulty...), never in the game world. Always visible, bottom centre: the mute / unmute button with the SAME animation as today (the little equalizer and its sound off / on text). Settings: music permanently off or on; a chosen soundtrack every time or back to random; under the soundtrack choices a note that all soundtracks are licensed free and safe to use in live streams on YouTube and elsewhere (each track's licence listed in THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md; the current title music is the owner's own, made with Suno AI Pro - full ownership, safe everywhere).", '4-6 h', ['none'], '156', False),
    ('158', "WITH 154/155 (owner, 2026-11-20) - Aircraft in the 3D world from the simulation: his A320 (the model parked at the gates now) stands in for EVERY aircraft type for now (no models for all types yet), for the AI airlines' traffic and for your own aircraft - the one you own at the start parks at a gate and, like every other, moves on its own schedule from the simulation: pushback, taxi, take-off, flying, landing, taxi in, parking. Lights animated as real: navigation lights on the wings, landing lights, taxi light by the nose wheel, strobes, beacon - each on and off at the right phase. When the moving aircraft are ready, the static parked A320s at the gates are removed. ON THE GROUND AND IN THE AIR (owner, 2026-11-20): every aircraft knows our own airfield - the runways, taxiways and stands we built (Data/airfield.json) - and follows the taxiway centre lines exactly; holds short at every holding point before a runway; the ATC tower gives the clearances (pushback, taxi, line up, take-off, landing) and makes traffic wait when needed; a flare just before touchdown; reverse thrust after landing; heat haze behind the engines only while they run; on a wet runway a big water spray behind it on take-off and landing, strong while thrust and speed are high, fading as the thrust comes back or it leaves the runway, then carries on as normal. CONTROL SURFACES (owner, 2026-11-20): spoilers up on landing; flaps (and slats) animated to the real A320 positions - take-off CONF 1+F / 2, landing CONF 3 / FULL, retracted in cruise. His A320 is one single mesh today: the flaps, slats and spoilers are first cut out of it as their own parts (his geometry kept exactly, the gaps behind them closed), hinged where the real ones move. Also the rudder and the ailerons (and the elevators): moving as the aircraft steers, banks and pitches - on the take-off roll, in turns, on approach and in the flare - cut out and hinged the same way. REAL MOTION (owner, 2026-11-20): the aircraft really turns and banks - heading changes along a curved path with the right bank angle, pitch and speed from real-world A320 data (turn rates, bank limits, rotation and climb angles, approach speeds and angles, taxi speeds and nose-wheel steering) - never sliding sideways or straight along one axis. EVERY NEW TYPE (when its own model comes): its own real-world data, its own moving parts animated and its lights placed the same way. CROSSWIND (owner, 2026-11-20): in a strong crosswind (the real weather's wind) the approach is flown in a crab - pointing partly into the wind at an angle to the runway, the path still on the centre line; just before touchdown the de-crab (rudder swings the nose straight, a little wing-low into the wind), the main gear touches first, the aircraft straightens on the runway, then the nose wheel comes down. Take-offs too: into-wind aileron on the roll, the crab after lift-off.", '10-14 h', ['field'], '154, 155', False),
    ('159', "POSSIBLE FEATURE - DECIDE AFTER THE AI WORK (owner, 2026-11-20; remind him when the AI rebuild is done) - A COPY of the whole airport somewhere else on the planet (the Iceland airport stays where it is, untouched): the terminal, every building and fitting, the airfield's custom ground, runways, taxiways, roads, light masts and the rest, placed as one block at a new latitude/longitude/heading. Follows by itself: sun, real weather, satellite pictures, the name on signs. To redo by tool there: the ground blend into the new terrain, the roads fitted (or re-planned), the land along the roads baked again; nothing in the buildings changes. High-resolution height data for the new area from that country's free national data where the licence allows a sold game (else the 30 m world data). OWNER 2026-11-20 (later): he will move his airport to Switzerland later (or copy it - to confirm then); all new work kept relocatable meanwhile. Earlier: he would like to choose a location in Switzerland later, with swissALTI3D (0.5-2 m, free for commercial use with credit to swisstopo).", '1-3 days', ['field'], '154', False),
    ('201', "WHEN HE ASKS FOR A TRAILER OR AN IN-GAME RECORDING (owner, 2026-11-21) - ASK HIM FIRST whether he wants cinematic camera work, as in the cinematic test (Tools/cinematic_test.sh): an auto-framed orbiting tracking shot - the camera follows the subject (tracking), swings slowly round it (arc / orbit), rises and sinks (crane / jib), keeps every moving part in frame (auto-framing) and eases into each move (damped follow). Offer the options: orbit, tracking, crane moves, close-ups on hands at work, wide shots; then record only the game window (video-game-window-only rule).", 'ask first', ['none'], '', False),
    ('202', "NUMBER 10 (owner, 2026-11-21) - The crew board BEFORE the passengers, as in real life: the jetway is extended to the aircraft's door only when the crew start boarding (never earlier in career/carrier mode), the cabin and flight crew walk through the terminal (staff route), through the jetway and aboard first; only then is the passenger boarding called. The jetway stays docked until ALL passengers are aboard (no-shows: the 15-minute rule), then pulls back to its rest position before the pushback request. (In the cinematic test the jetway is docked from the start so nothing holds the test up.) Builds on PaxFlow (boarding, fade in the jetway until the cabin exists), SimTraffic (jetway + BoardingDone gate), staff daily life (to-do 1 OWNER OVERRIDE (2026-11-24): required before the full test and before publishing, with 209.16) and crew control (134).", '4-6 h', ['none'], '', False),
    ('203', "DOOR CONTROLS (owner, 2026-11-21) - The player controls the terminal's doors: open / close / lock each door or a whole group (the entrance sliding doors, security, gates, staff and service doors, roller doors), from the PC, the tablet and on the spot (E / X at the door's panel). Today they work by themselves: the entrance doors stay open while passengers are about and close only when the airport is empty, the security doors stay open until the last passenger is through, the roller doors open for vehicles. A forced close or a lock overrides that (passengers then wait or are turned away, and the boards show it); real rules for fire exits (never locked from inside) and the night closing of the terminal.", '3-5 h', ['none'], '', False),
    ('204', "PASSENGER ACCESSORIES - MODELS (owner, 2026-11-21; do not make the Blender models until he says) - Our own models from reference photos, each with its Art/<Object>/<Object>.blend: a BACKPACK (a few colours, straps fitted to the MetaHuman shoulders, no clipping when walking or sitting - set on the floor by the seat when it would clip), wireless EARBUDS and over-ear HEADPHONES. Then on passengers: ~30% backpacks, ~25% earbuds or headphones (worn all the time). The phone (everyone) and tablet (~10%) props with the IK grip use the existing player phone/tablet models and are already built (PaxProps).", '3-4 h', ['none'], '', False),
    ('205', "BEFORE THE NEXT PUBLISH, RIGHT AFTER THE PERFORMANCE RUN (owner, 2026-11-25; with 140) - EVERY PASSENGER OF EVERY FLIGHT WALKING IN THE TERMINAL (hundreds at once) WITHOUT LOSING QUALITY. WHY IT IS NUMBER 1: today only ~36 passengers per flight can walk (the rest are numbers), because every walker is a full MetaHuman (face, hair strands, IK) - too heavy for hundreds; this is also what makes the frame rate drop when many walk (helps to-do 149). HOW (the trick Epic used in The Matrix Awakens - layers of detail): (1) close to the camera (~20 m) the full MetaHuman as now - face, real hair, suitcase grip, phone, idle moves, stepping aside; (2) further out (20-60 m) the same person as ONE merged mesh with hair cards, a simpler face and fewer animation updates; (3) far away (60 m+) cheap copies with the walk/idle/sit animation baked into a texture and played by the graphics card (Unreal's AnimToTexture), hundreds to thousands for the cost of a few MetaHumans, made from OUR MetaHumans with colour variations and their bags. A person moves up and down the layers as the camera comes near - the swap is not seen. All passengers become light data in Unreal's crowd system (Mass, already started in TerminalCrowd/PassengerFragments) walking on lanes through the terminal (ZoneGraph) instead of each one searching its own path. Interactions (bag on the weigh belt, desk, jetway fade) stay exact for the near ones, simplified far away. Then the passenger cap per flight goes: everyone on the flight walks. Built in parts: (a) passengers onto Mass + lanes, (b) baking our MetaHumans' animations, (c) the layer swap, (d) the far crowd copies + bags, then a stress walk (frame rate measured, Full HD + 4K + his screen).", '10-14 h', ['none'], '', False),
    ('206', "GO (owner, 2026-11-23 evening: have the ground crew in, you can hire new ones; their clothes come later) - YOUR HIRED STAFF ARE THE PEOPLE IN THE 3D AIRPORT. WHY: today the ground work (tug drivers, pushback handlers, wing walkers, the GPU handler, the baggage tractor drivers and helpers, the belt loader crew) is done by a FIXED stand-in team of ~16 built-in MetaHumans, there only so the vehicles could be built and tested - hiring someone in the staff screens does not add anyone to it, and the team works even if you hired nobody. WHAT: the stand-in team is removed; every person you hire becomes their own MetaHuman in 3D (their name, face, role, shift) and does the duties of that role (ground staff: tugs, GPU, bags, belt loader, wing walking; later security, check-in, fuel, catering, cleaning, fire - as those jobs exist); no one hired for a job = the job is not done and the flights are delayed (logged, shown on the boards); more staff = jobs done in parallel. Pilots and cabin crew walk and board too (to-do 202). Builds on to-do 116 (staff daily life: home in town, commute, 8-h rota) and leads to 134 (taking control of any crew member). PART 1 OF 3 BUILT 2026-11-23, NOT RUN (off by default, adp.HiredCrew): the hired people themselves - face, shift, staff door, standing by - and the pool the vehicles will ask; part 2 the vehicles' crew, part 3 the hiring screens: docs/Hired-Crew-Plan.md.", '8-12 h', ['none'], '', False),
    ('207', "MONDAY 23 NOVEMBER - FIRST, before publishing the 11-21 batch (owner, 2026-11-21) - The cinematic test's passengers must board THE aircraft at the gate: in the 11-21 evening test the 20 test passengers were made a moment before the simulation had registered aircraft CH-AEF's turnaround at stand 9, so they fell back to gate 2 with no aircraft linked - CH-AEF then found nobody waiting for it and pushed back while they walked. Fix (PaxFlow MakeTestRuns): wait until a real turnaround exists at a jetway gate and link the test departure (and the test arrival) to that aircraft and its gate; if none appears, hold rather than guess a gate. Then the cinematic test (Tools/cinematic_test.sh): passengers walk into that aircraft's jetway and fade, and only then does it ask ATC for pushback.", '0.5 h', ['none'], '', False),
    ('208', "NUMBER 5 (owner, 2026-11-22) - AIRCRAFT LIVERIES - YOUR OWN PAINT ON THE A320, MADE IN THE GAME (owner, 2026-11-22). (1) A PAINT LAYER under the aircraft's own details: door outlines, panel lines, rivets, windows, hatches, small markings and shading always stay on top; paint never covers glass, tyres, the landing gear, bare-metal leading edges or the engine exhaust (masked). (2) A CLEAN PAINT TEMPLATE (a second UV unwrap): the fuselage as one strip (left, right, top, belly), the tail fin both sides, the stabilisers, the wings top and bottom, the winglets and each engine nacelle - every part labelled, easy to tell apart; the MOVING PARTS (ailerons, flaps, spoilers, rudder, elevators) keep all their detail and get their own places on the template lined up with the surface next to them, so a stripe runs straight across a flap and moves with it. (3) THE LIVERY MAKER IN THE GAME, as simple as possible: a colour per part, stripes, shapes, logos, pictures and text (the airline name), automatic left/right mirroring with text reading the right way on both sides, a live 3D preview, save / load / share liveries, and a downloadable template image to paint outside the game and load back in. (3b) ONLY YOUR OWN AIRCRAFT: the livery maker paints only the aircraft of YOUR airline (the ones you buy or lease) - never the AI airlines' aircraft (maybe a mod later, not in the game). WHERE: an app on the in-game PCs (your office, Operations) and on your in-game TABLET - NOT on the phone (too small to work on). (4) BACKUPS: the player saves a backup of a livery (its layout file) on their own PC whenever they want and loads it to get the layout back (revert); up to 10 backups kept per livery; and ONE AUTOMATIC BACKUP is always made the moment a model's default colours are first changed to their own. (4b) WEAR AND DIRT STAY ON TOP: the aircraft's wear and tear (chipped paint, scratches, exhaust stains, streaks, grime) and its dirt decals are their own layer ABOVE the livery - a new livery never erases or covers them; they show on the new paint the same way (the dirt also keeps building up and washing off as it does today). (5) REAL RULE: the aircraft's registration (e.g. CH-ABC) is always written on the fuselage and under the wing automatically, on top of any livery (ICAO Annex 7), so a design can never hide it.", '12-20 h', ['none'], '', False),
    ('209', "OWNER'S OK STEP (2026-11-24): the cabin with its IK seats is built and placed inside the Airbus, then pictures; only after the owner confirms it looks good does it go into the game (other jobs go on meanwhile). The passengers keep their animations (walk, sit, stand up, idle, seat IK); new only: the way from the jetway through the door and aisle to the own seat, and a few cabin moves (bag into the bin, sidling into the row, letting others pass, belt). OWNER 2026-11-24: EVERY SEAT IN THE CABIN CAN BE SAT ON WITH IK, BY YOU AND THE PEOPLE - every passenger seat, the cabin crew's jump seats, the pilots' and observer seats, and the toilet seats; seat rules as everywhere (E, taken = blocked, hands on armrests, feet on the floor, belt on). Stands for every future aircraft too. OWNER 2026-11-24: ALL MOVING PARTS ANIMATED and doing what they need to do when they need to do it (see the aircraft cabin standard): passenger, service and cargo doors opened by hand by the crew at real speed, slides stay armed/disarmed, overhead bins, cockpit and toilet doors, galley trolleys and latches, seat belts, tables, backrests, window blinds; outside gear, gear doors, flaps, slats, spoilers, ailerons, elevators, rudder, engine fans and reversers, lights. Same for every apron vehicle. use a REAL Airbus A320 cabin layout diagram (Airbus airport planning document: doors, galleys, lavatories, seat pitch, bins); if the aircraft is out of scale, SCALE IT TO ITS REAL PROPORTIONS (go given, no waiting for pictures), then re-measure every vehicle, jetway and service point on it. IN THE BIG RELEASE (after the wings, roof and remote stands) - the walk-in CABIN fits exactly inside our A320 and looks like a real A320 cabin in high detail (not overdone): modelled in Blender, all parts connected, no seams between cabin and outer shell, windows too (one connected 3D wall, inner and outer pane in one reveal), seats 3 + 3 on each side of one aisle, overhead bins that really open so passengers put their bags in, cockpit door closed and unlocked (can be opened). - NUMBER 11 - NOT BEFORE MONDAY 23 NOVEMBER (owner, 2026-11-22) - THE A320 AT ITS REAL SIZE, THEN A PERFECT CABIN INSIDE IT. (1) Measure the current A320 model (Art/A320) against the real aircraft (fuselage 3.95 m wide outside, cabin 3.70 m wide inside, cabin ~27.5 m long, length 37.57 m, span 35.8 m, height 11.76 m) and SCALE THE WHOLE AIRCRAFT TO ITS REAL SIZE - WITHOUT BREAKING ITS GRAPHICS (owner): every texture, decal, light, livery/UV, normal map and the moving parts' pivots must look exactly as now, no stretching, no seams, no flicker (find_zfight), no missing slots; check it close up before and after. FIRST SHOW HIM how it looks at real size (pictures next to the current one, at a stand with the jetway, tug and GPU) and WAIT FOR HIS OK before going on. Everything measured on the current model then gets re-measured: jetway docking (door L1 height and place), stand stop lines, the GPU socket, the belt loader at the bulk hold, the tug's towbar point, gear and light positions. (1b) EVERY DOOR OF THE AIRCRAFT OPENS (owner, 2026-11-22), split out of the model as their own moving parts with real hinges and real motion: the four passenger doors (L1, L2, R1, R2 - they swing out and forward along the fuselage), the forward and aft cargo doors (open upward, outward), the BULK HOLD door, the service and avionics hatches, so ground crew can reach them. The BULK HOLD DOOR (and the cargo doors) are opened by a ground crew member who PHYSICALLY WALKS UP to the door, reaches its handle (hand IK on the handle, the arm lifting the door as it swings) and opens it like a real handler, when the belt loader comes to it - no door ever opens by itself, at the REAL WORLD speed (the time a real A320's door takes - find it in the Airbus documentation), and the belt loader's boom and the cart train only start their work once the door is fully open; closed again at the real speed when loading is done, before the pushback. The passenger doors open for the jetway (L1), the catering (R1/R2) and the crew in the real order. (2) Then the cabin, built to the real-size shell from slices of the model's inside (floor, walls, ceiling, the windows and the four doors): 150-180 seats at real pitch with a real aisle, overhead bins, ceiling panels and lights, sidewall panels with window frames lined up exactly with the model's windows, front and rear galleys, lavatories, crew seats, door insides, the cockpit door - our own models from reference photos of real A320 cabins, instanced + Nanite; fitted with no gaps and nothing through the skin; seats and shades seen through the windows from outside. (3) BOARDING INTO THE CABIN (owner, 2026-11-22 - the standard for EVERY aircraft cabin made in the future): once the jetway is docked, the passenger door opens at the real aircraft's speed (real procedure: the cabin crew open it from inside after the jetway is in place and the agent gives the all-clear - tell him if a real rule differs), and stays open; you can see into the cabin through it. Passengers walk through the jetway, through the door, along the aisle - like walking into a bus - to THEIR OWN seat (row and letter from their booking) and sit down (sitting IK). Some (not all) put a backpack, bag or coat in the overhead bin above their row first (hand IK, the bin opens and closes); others keep it under the seat in front. THE JETWAY OPERATOR (a ground crew member): stands at the jetway's console inside the cab while it docks (the HMI, to-do 115), then WAITS at the door - he cannot open it from outside - until the cabin crew open it from inside, stays a short moment (the real hand-over: a word with the crew, the canopy and the floor checked), then moves on to his next task; ground crew may come and go through the jetway's SERVICE DOOR (the stair down to the apron) and through any other terminal door they need (staff doors, gate doors, the ramp services doors - with their badge, as in real life), whichever way is best for the next task. After boarding the operator returns, the cabin crew close the door at real speed, then he pulls the jetway back to its rest position. No more fading. The crew OWNER OVERRIDE (2026-11-24): the cabin (once the owner has approved the pictures) and passengers actually boarding/deplaning into it in full 3D are now required before the terminal's full test and before publishing. board first (to-do 202). CABIN ANNOUNCEMENTS (owner, 2026-11-24): once the cabin is built, the cabin crew make real cabin announcements at every real step of the flight (boarding, safety demo, doors-to-arrival, taxi, takeoff, cruise, descent, landing, deplaning), in the real order and at the real times, following real airline regulations.", '15-25 h', ['none'], '', False),
    ('210', "MONDAY 23 NOVEMBER - FIRST TASK AFTER THE CINEMATIC TEST (owner, 2026-11-22; important) - CHECK-IN AGENTS. A new hiring role 'Passenger Service Agent' (check-in, bag drop, gate and boarding - not ground crew; real employers are the airline or a handler like Swissport) in the staff screens with its own pay. Stand-in agents until to-do 206: they arrive ~20 min before check-in opens (log in, printers), staff their airline's desks (desks by load: ~1 per 40-50 passengers), take turns for breaks so a desk never stands empty, check passengers in. THE DOCUMENT HAND-OVER, REAL AND VISIBLE (owner, 2026-11-22): the passenger takes the passport (and the booking / phone with the ticket) out of a pocket or bag, holds it up and hands it across the desk; the agent reaches, takes it (both hands on the document by IK - fingers closed on its edge, the document passing from one hand to the other at the meeting point), looks at it, types, then hands the passport back together with the printed boarding pass; the passenger takes them back and puts them away - every grip and hand-over by hand IK so it looks natural, the documents real small models (passport, boarding pass, phone) that you can clearly see moving between the hands. The same hand-over at the gate scan, security's boarding-pass check and passport control (to-do 211/214). Check-in opens T-150 (long-haul T-180) and CLOSES at T-45 (short-haul): the desk shows CLOSED, the queue at that moment is served, latecomers are sent to the airline's service desk (rebooked or refused - no bag flies without its passenger). Then the agents walk to the gate and work the boarding: scan each boarding pass (beep) and check ID before the jetway, the boarding calls and the final call. Plan: docs/Departure-Flow-Plan.md.", '6-8 h', ['none'], '', False),
    ('211', "THE DEPARTURE FLOW - ONE TIMELINE PER FLIGHT, EVERYTHING IN SYNC (owner, 2026-11-22) - the full plans with every step, real times and what is still missing: docs/Departure-Flow-Plan.md/.pdf and docs/Arrival-Flow-Plan.md/.pdf (the arrival's missing pieces too: stand allocation in Operations, docking guidance / marshaller, chocks and cones with the beacon rule, transfers, border entry + customs, priority bags and real first/last-bag times, lost and found, oversized belt, PRM help, meeters and greeters, the night stop). Extend Turnaround into one timeline per flight from the agents' arrival to the take-off (check-in, security, airside, gate, crew report and briefing, cabin checks, boarding, final call, gate close, no-show bag offload, load sheet, doors, jetway, de-icing with holdover time, push, engine start, taxi, take-off), every step a planned time from a data table and the steps it waits for; delays spread down the chain and onto the boards. Then build the missing real-world pieces in the plan's order: security details (boarding-pass scan, trays, random checks), catering / fuel / de-icing trucks and cleaners, the cabin checks and load sheet (with 209 / 202), border control for non-Schengen flights, PRM passengers, online check-in / bag drop / kiosks.", '30-50 h', ['none'], '', False),
    ('212', "THE MISSING MODELS FOR REAL DEPARTURES AND ARRIVALS (owner, 2026-11-22) - every one our own model from reference photos, blueprint first and his OK before building, instanced + Nanite, the pushback tug's quality for vehicles. FIRST A SPACE CHECK: a blueprint of the terminal showing where each new piece goes (kiosks and bag-drop machines in the Grand Hall's front zone, the service / ticket desk, boarding-pass gates at the two security doors, trays in the lanes, passport booths + e-gates and customs channels for a non-Schengen area, the lost-and-found desk + oversized belt + trolley bays in the claim wing, an arrivals hall for meeters and greeters) - and where the terminal is too small, a proposal to extend it - for his OK. VEHICLES: catering truck (lifting box), fuel truck / hydrant dispenser, de-icing truck (boom and basket), water truck, toilet-service truck, ULD container loader + ULD containers, passenger stairs truck, ambulift, cleaning van. APRON: chocks, safety cones, docking guidance displays at every stand (or marshaller wands + vests), engine and pitot covers. TERMINAL: self-service kiosks, bag-drop machines, service / ticket desk, boarding-pass gates with glass flaps, security trays, passport booths + e-gates, customs channels (green/red), lost-and-found desk, oversized-items belt, baggage trolleys, gate scanners, wheelchairs, meeters' name signs. ADDED 2026-11-22 for the arrivals (to-do 216): ARRIVALS DUTY FREE shops in both claim halls (before customs), ATM + currency exchange, a kiosk / SIM-card shop, the information desk in the west arrivals area too, a TAXI RANK with its shelter and sign on the forecourt, PARKING PAY MACHINES at the car park exits. PEOPLE'S ITEMS: boarding passes and passports to hand over, coats (backpacks + earphones are to-do 204).", '30-50 h', ['none'], '', False),
    ('213', "NUMBER 1 - BLUEPRINT ACCEPTED 2026-11-22 (docs/Blueprints/Terminal-Changes; with a double-skin front at x 114-156 so the F2 offices keep their windows) - v2 APPROVED 2026-11-23, BEING BUILT IN 5 PARTS (docs/Blueprints/Terminal-Changes/v2; the east wing is open grass today, so this is new building under the existing wave roof) - A PROPER BLUEPRINT OF THE TERMINAL CHANGES for the missing departure/arrival pieces (to-do 212, the quick sketch Blueprints/Terminal/Space-Check-Sketch.png): exact measurements, BEFORE and AFTER side by side, so he sees exactly what changes. RULES: the Grand Hall must NOT get smaller (it would be crowded) - if anything, more room; every existing room and building part stays (moved if needed, never removed); no gate, stand or jetway moves; it must still look good. METHOD: (1) measure every area today and its capacity by the real IATA level-of-service standard (m2 per person in check-in queues, security, gate lounges, the claim hall) for the busiest hour of the schedule; (2) list each new piece with its real size (kiosks, bag-drop machines, service desk, boarding-pass gates, passport booths + e-gates both ways, customs channels, lost and found, oversized belt, trolley bays, an arrivals hall); (3) place them FIRST in space that is free today - the empty ground-floor EAST WING (plan x 156-551, 52 m deep, 'empty for now'), the entrance vestibules, the lounge's edge - and only then extend the building on the landside; (4) an area table before/after: the Grand Hall's area the same or larger, the queue space kept clear; (4b) THE EAST WING TO ITS FULLEST (owner): a plan for the whole empty ground-floor east wing (x 156-551, 52 m deep) - what goes where; if the Grand Hall grows or a building goes beside it, the baggage belts (check-in tunnel, the departure baggage block) are re-routed to match; FROM THE OUTSIDE (passenger side) any new or bigger hall gets the SAME tall glass facade as the Grand Hall's big window, running all the way up to the curved roof perfectly, with the same pillars (columns) up to the roof where needed, and the roof line continued so it looks like one building; (4c) THE SAME STANDARD INSIDE (owner): every new or changed area - the East Hall, the non-Schengen side of gates 13-16, passport control in and out, the arrivals corridor and mezzanine, the non-Schengen claim hall, customs, the promenade and the coach station - is finished as beautifully as the Grand Hall: the same marble floors (M_Marble) and dark wood where the gates have it, the same glass to the roof and tree pillars, the same lighting, signs, screens and seating, shops and plants - never plain or grey; (5) 3D concept renders of the changed areas and of the outside from the forecourt; then WAIT FOR HIS OK, then build (to-do 212).", '3-4 h for the blueprint', ['none'], '', False),
    ('214', "MONDAY 23 NOVEMBER - WITH TO-DO 213 (owner, 2026-11-22) - SWING GATES. FIRST a blueprint of how the layout changes (before / after, Floor 2 and the arrivals corridor, 4K, black) for his OK, then build it. Gates that switch per flight between Schengen and non-Schengen: each swing gate gets two door sets (one from the Schengen walkway, one from the non-Schengen side / arrivals corridor) - only one open at a time; before each flight staff lock one set and open the other, and the gate screen and signs change ('London - non-Schengen'); a gate never switches while passengers of the last flight are still there (swept empty first); arriving non-Schengen passengers at a swing gate go into the closed arrivals corridor to passport control in, never onto the Schengen walkway; Operations decides per flight which gates are which, and the boards, announcements and passenger routes follow. ENFORCED: a Schengen passenger can never pass the passport wall or a swing gate's non-Schengen side - their route never goes there AND the e-gates / booths / doors only open for a valid non-Schengen boarding pass after the passport check (anyone else is stopped and turned back); the rest of the wall is solid glass. The player passes through staff doors with his badge. STAFF + SYSTEMS (real practice): the door sets are run by the airport's access-control system (locked, alarmed, on CCTV, switched from Operations), but a SECURITY OFFICER does the switch on the spot - sweeps the gate area to confirm it is empty, checks both door sets, then the system switches; the gate agents (Passenger Service Agents, to-do 210) work the boarding at the desk; an alarm brings security if a door is forced or left open.", '10-15 h + blueprint 1-2 h', ['none'], '', False),
    ('215', "MONDAY 23 NOVEMBER (owner, 2026-11-22) - PASSENGERS ON FLOOR 2 LIVE THERE, NOT JUST WAIT. After the escalators: the MAJORITY (~60-70 %) go straight to their own gate area; the rest first go somewhere else that is open to them - the tax-free shop, a shop, the food court, a cafe or bar, the toilets, a window to watch the aircraft - for a realistic stay (5-25 min, browsing, buying, eating, queueing at a till), then to their gate. While waiting at the gate, some GET UP AGAIN now and then (a chance every 10-30 min): the toilet, a coffee, a shop, a stretch walk along the walkway, a phone call standing by the window - then back to a seat (maybe a different free one, seat rules) or a wall. Families and groups move together. Everyone is back at their gate in time for the boarding call (the late ones hurry or run, as built); the time they need to get back is part of the choice (no shop trip right before boarding). Real numbers from airport dwell-time studies. Uses the idle moves, phones and seat IK already built.", '3-4 h', ['none'], '', False),
    ('216', "MONDAY 23 NOVEMBER, AFTER 215 (owner, 2026-11-22) - ARRIVING PASSENGERS: EVERY STEP OUT OF THE TERMINAL, WITH STOPS ON THE WAY. The way out, in the real order: off the aircraft through the jetway -> along the walkway (some stop at the TOILETS) -> non-Schengen flights: PASSPORT CONTROL IN (booths/e-gates, the document hand-over by IK) -> down to the claim hall -> some visit ARRIVALS DUTY FREE (allowed at Swiss airports; in the claim hall before customs) or the toilets -> take a BAGGAGE TROLLEY (some) -> wait at their flight's belt (stand, lean, phone, idle moves) -> take their OWN bag off the belt (built) -> a missing bag: to LOST AND FOUND to report it -> oversize items from the oversize belt -> CUSTOMS green or red (random checks with a valid reason) -> through the one-way EXIT DOORS (no way back) -> the ARRIVALS HALL: meeters and greeters waiting (hugs, name signs), and some passengers stop at the cafe, an ATM / currency exchange, a SIM-card / kiosk shop, the information desk or CAR RENTAL -> leave: to the CAR PARK (some pay at the parking machine first), the TAXI rank, the BUS / COACH station, later the train. Shares (most go straight out, a minority stop) and stay times from real airport studies; families and groups move together. Each stop's model comes from to-do 212.", '4-6 h', ['none'], '', False),
    ('217', "DONE 2026-11-23 at Chablais (164 spaces lost instead of 224, 3 818 left; barriers not animated yet) - WITH TO-DO 213 (owner, 2026-11-23) - THE CAR PARK'S ENTRY AND EXIT, approved: blueprint TCv2-2f OPTION 1, a give-way junction at the back onto the road to the town (x 120), 2 entry + 2 exit barrier lanes, new inside lanes, footways to the zebras, pay machines. Nothing needed may be destroyed: as few of the 224 spaces lost as possible, zebras, coach stands, walkers' routes and the passengers' spawn spaces keep working.", 'done', ['none'], '', True),
    ('218', "BUILT 2026-11-23 (every Floor 2 gate) - CURVED GATE WINDOWS on EVERY Floor 2 gate window (gates 1-4, 9, 11, 13-16) and every future gate, never the view bays: the 3 m true circle of docs/Blueprints/Gate-Arc, jetways not moved. FIX in it: the black patch under the curve beside the jetway corridor (docs/Blueprints/Gate-Arc/Owner/2026-11-23_black-underside.jpg; SlotSoffit in ga_scene.py) gets the same white as the underside next to it. Then the gate room box, passenger paths, seats and holomap follow the curve.", '8-14 h', ['none'], '', False),
    ('219', "LATER, WHEN THE OWNER SAYS (2026-11-23) - SWITZERLAND ONLY, ICELAND HIDDEN (no files deleted): a new game puts you straight in Switzerland, with no airport choice; Iceland's airport, its airport code, its name and its place can never be seen or found anywhere in the game, but every file is KEPT. RESEARCH FIRST: list everything Chablais takes from the Iceland airport (shared airfield data, layout, models, coordinates, names) and make Switzerland its own complete thing, so nothing in Switzerland ever goes away. Old saves may break. Nothing is hidden until the research is done and the owner has the list.", '6-10 h', ['none'], '', False),
    ('220', "AFTER the AI passengers + ground crew work and the terminal is fully built (owner, 2026-11-23) - REAL-WORLD WEATHER, ALWAYS: the game tracks the weather at the airport right now (Chablais) and you see it in the game: cloud cover, type and height, rain, snow, fog and visibility, wind, thunder, temperature, wet and drying ground. It must NOT be possible to change the weather in the game: the test bar's weather buttons and every other weather choice go (the overcast setting does not reach the clouds today: fix with it). When the weather service cannot be reached: the last known real weather is kept, never a made-up one.", '12-18 h', ['none'], '', False),
    ('221', "WITH 220 (owner, 2026-11-23) - SKY AND CLOUDS MADE FOR GAMING, NOT FOR FILM: today the sky and clouds run with the engine's cinematic (movie-making) settings (about 10-12 ms of a frame at 3840x1080, seen 2026-11-20). The standard becomes game settings that still look extremely good (fewer ray samples with clean upsampling, spread over frames, cloud shadows and sky light cheaply updated, a lower real cloud layer). SETTINGS gets CLOUD QUALITY (Low / Medium / High / Epic) plus a separate FILMIC CINEMATIC CLOUDS switch that brings today's look back; its standard is OFF. Side-by-side pictures of today's and the new look for his OK. The one saver switch 'Fast clouds' is folded in. A player's other saved settings are never touched.", '6-10 h', ['none'], '', False),
    ('222', "WITH 220 (owner, 2026-11-23) - RAIN IN THE DISTANCE, RAINBOWS, NORTHERN LIGHTS. (1) RAIN YOU SEE COMING: where it really rains now (the weather radar's picture, moved with the real wind) grey rain (or snow) shafts hang under their own clouds in the 3D world, kilometres away, over the mountains, and come nearer until they reach you; then the drops fall around you, the ground gets wet from that edge on, the sound grows with the distance; it leaves the same way. (2) RAINBOWS: only when real - the sun lower than 42 degrees behind you and lit rain in front - at the right angle and size, a fainter second bow outside, fading with the rain. (3) NORTHERN LIGHTS by the real space weather (the official aurora forecast / Kp): in Switzerland only on the rare strong storms when they truly show, low and red on the northern horizon; otherwise where they are normally seen, e.g. from your aircraft far north at night. Radar source: MeteoSwiss / another free source whose licence allows a paid game; checked first.", '16-24 h', ['none'], '', False),
    ('223', "DECIDED 2026-11-25 (over the older Kokoro words below): passengers AND staff / crew = CHATTERBOX, the airport announcer = KOKORO. Not before the publish that is being made; right after it, and if it doesn't fit before the weekly limit it is NUMBER 1 on Monday 2026-11-30. The dialogue is really dynamic: random what can be asked, they answer accordingly - the city they are in, their experience in Switzerland, how they look forward to where they go and a reason why. AFTER the AI passengers + crew work (owner, 2026-11-23; with to-do 69's conduct) - EVERYONE SPEAKS OUT LOUD: whenever a passenger or staff member says something you HEAR it, with the game's own built-in AI voice (Kokoro, offline): passenger and you; passenger and check-in / gate agent, stewardess, pilot, shop or cafe staff, security, passport officer, police; staff and security among themselves; and misconduct - passengers arguing or fighting with words, security and police with them (tones by the conduct plan: calm, mild, aggressive). CHATTER: standing very close to someone, by random and not often, they say something on their own - never vulgar: daily life, their journey, what they think of this airport, of airlines and aircraft, the weather, the food, a delay (about their OWN flight, the real state of things). Each person keeps one voice (age, man/woman, accent), lips moving, turned to whom they speak; by place: distance, walls, hall echo; subtitles optional; never two people over each other at your ear; a voice volume slider. RANDOM: not all passengers chatter or get into arguments - only some, by each one's own nature and chance; but EVERY passenger you approach and interact with (E / X) talks back in a real dialogue (to-do 69). VOICE ENGINE (owner, 2026-11-23: download allowed to F:, it must ship with the game and run offline): The BEST Chatterbox for each use (compare the newest: 500M with the emotion dial, Turbo, newer ones): emotions, anger AND normal talk; Chatterbox (MIT) as the live voice on the processor, tuned - smaller number format (int8/q4 ONNX), voices prepared ahead, sentence by sentence streaming, work ahead and cache, low thread priority on set cores, ready-made lines (500M, emotion dial) made ahead and shipped for FRUSTRATED and ANGRY speech, which must be VERY GOOD (owner): levels calm / frustrated / angry / violent, many wordings, every line checked by ear and machine, bad ones remade, real recordings if the dial is not enough; EVERY PERSON THEIR OWN VOICE, kept from calm to angry (about 200 licensed voices: LJ Speech, VCTK, others after a licence check + mixes; about 40 with the full anger set; nobody near you shares a voice; staff have fixed voices); CHATTERBOX IS THE ABSOLUTE NUMBER 1 VOICE; never switched off by itself: if it has trouble keeping up, a PROMPT asks 'Chatterbox has problems on this PC. Switch to Kokoro?' (Switch / Keep Chatterbox): the prompt never blocks or freezes the game, works with mouse, keyboard and gamepad, gives the pointer, closes at once on a click, can be moved past with Esc, and REMEMBERS the choice for good (saved in the settings, never asked again unless the player changes it there); tested that it opens, clicks and closes in airport mode, menus and while talking; Settings always has the choice Voice engine: Chatterbox (standard) / Kokoro; Kokoro stays for announcements; try it first alone + beside the game and let him hear it. Far away = murmur, no speech made (cheap: lines made ahead on another thread, a cache, a few voices at once).", '16-24 h', ['none'], '', False),
    ('224', "DONE 2026-11-23 (a fair woman with a blonde low ponytail, Epic's own hair, a person already made, built as close as him; turned a little so it shows) - TODAY, BEFORE THE RELEASE (owner, 2026-11-23) - 'WHO ARE YOU?': the woman shown (SHE) becomes a EUROPEAN-LOOKING woman with a PONYTAIL (another of the MetaHumans already in the game, or her look changed; real hair, lit as well as HE). Only the default figure of that screen: every look stays available in the creator afterwards. Look at it on 32:9 and 16:9.", '2-3 h', ['none'], '', True),
    ('225', "DONE 2026-11-23 (the belts run in clear gaps between narrower desks, the old scale boxes are out, the numbers read right for the passengers; the tape in front is gone - short barrier lines between two airlines' desks instead) - TODAY (owner, 2026-11-23; picture docs/Pictures/Owner/2026-11-23_checkin-gap-flicker.jpg) - GRAND HALL CHECK-IN: no flicker or overlapping parts between any two check-in desks (the belt box in the gap stands through the desk beside it; every gap, all desks 1-24, the new belts too); the blue tape right in front of the desks is not natural: remove it, or put it where a real queue line would be.", 'done', ['none'], '', True),
    ('226', "LATER, NOT ANYTIME SOON (owner, 2026-11-23: information only, do not build yet) - THE GAME WORLD IS NEVER SEEN BEFORE THE LOADING IS DONE: no flicker or glimpse of the world behind a loading screen, the title screen, 'Who are you?' or the settings - ever, at every change between them. 'Who are you?' (and the creator steps) never shows after any loading: it comes before. The only loading the player sees is when the game starts into the airport: everything is loaded then, and the shaders are made then too if they are not made yet; the world shows only after all of it is done. When everything is loaded, 5 SECONDS pass (instead of today's 4, 'Loading complete - Get ready for takeoff') before you are in the game.", '6-10 h', ['none'], '', False),
    ('227', "DONE 2026-11-23 (the interface shows only while you really are in the airport; the new-game test looks at every frame before it) - BEFORE THE RELEASE (owner, 2026-11-23) - NO IN-GAME INTERFACE BEFORE THE GAME IS LAUNCHED: the holographic map showed over the character creator. Nothing of the airport's own interface (map, compass, bars, notes, buttons, ...) may show on the title screen, the menus and settings from there, the site question, 'Who are you?', the creator's steps or the loading; it shows only once you are in the airport. Checked by the new-game test on every one of those screens.", '1-2 h', ['none'], '', True),
    ('228', "LATER (owner, 2026-11-23; with 32a/32b, do not build yet) - CHARACTER CREATOR: PRESET FACES TO CHOOSE FROM. To begin with 20 faces (10 women and 10 men unless the owner says otherwise); skin tones per face still to be decided by the owner. Built ahead from Epic's preset faces, each about 4 minutes of PC time (20 faces in one skin tone each = about 1 h 20 min; in 4 tones = about 5.5 h). More faces can follow.", '4-6 h + PC time', ['people'], '32b', False),
    ('229', "LATER, WITH 205 (owner asked, 2026-11-23) - NO TWO PASSENGERS LOOK LIKE COPIES: today each passenger is one of 58 built people picked at random, so the same person can be seen twice. Nobody the same in view of each other; with hundreds walking, the clothes, colours, hair and body vary as well, and more people are built if needed (about 4 min each).", '2-4 h', ['people'], '205', False),
    ('230', "THE FIRST PART DONE 2026-11-23 (wife and husband beside today's He and She both European-looking; the rest with the creator) - SPOUSE OF THE SAME ETHNICITY AS YOU BY DEFAULT (owner, 2026-11-23): when you choose married, the spouse shown first has the same ethnicity as you - a wife for him, a husband for her, both ways round - and you can change it. Now, before the release (with 224): the spouse shown beside today's fixed He and She matches them. With the creator (32a-c, 228): it follows the face and skin you chose, and the spouse's own creator step lets you change ethnicity, face and skin freely.", '20 min now, rest with 32c', ['people'], '224', False),
    ('231', "LATER (owner, 2026-11-23: to-do list only; after 69's conduct and 223's voices) - INJURIES AND MEDICS: when somebody is injured in the airport, from a fight or anything else (not a criminal who got the stun gun), there is the option to notify the hospital. (1) The medics then arrive: the men themselves spawn out of sight, always at an angle the player isn't looking at, and walk to the scene with a stretcher. (2) At the scene they speak and ask questions that fit what happened, and the injured one answers; everything said is always heard, spoken by the game's own AI voice (to-do 223). (3) The injured person is brought onto the stretcher and really rolled the whole way out of the terminal, every metre, nothing skipped; from the upper floors by a lift down to the first floor, without any problems (check that every lift takes a stretcher with two medics, and that each floor has one; make it fit where it doesn't). (4) Outside stands the medics' vehicle: the person is put in and they drive off with them to the hospital. WHERE THE HOSPITAL IS: not decided yet - REMIND THE OWNER to decide; until then they only drive away. Parts: the injuries and the option, the medics and their vehicle (modelled, own livery), the stretcher with its IK (lifting, rolling, loading), the lifts, the conversation, the drive.", '16-24 h', ['people', 'voice'], '69, 223', False),
    ('232', "BEFORE THE RELEASE, THE VERY LAST JOB OF 2026-11-23 (owner, 2026-11-23) - COACH AT THE ZEBRA CROSSING: a coach leaving its stand with its passengers aboard has to cross the zebra crossing; with a passenger on the crossing it hung for a few seconds and then drove on; sometimes the game froze at that point, sometimes it went on, froze and went on again, or froze and then crashed. Find whether the wait, the freeze and the crash are one cause (and whether it is also the coaches' IK test's stuck step 7), and fix it: a smooth stop, a wait for as long as somebody is on the crossing, then on, with no freeze.", '2-4 h', ['people'], '', False),
    ('233', "BEFORE THE RELEASE (owner, 2026-11-23, part of the passengers' work) - THE BLUE CHAIRS: passengers sitting on the blue chairs (and on the seats in the gates' curved windows: turned round too) face the wrong way, their backs away from the backrest. They must sit with their backs against the backrest, on every chair of every kind (also the coaches' seats), with the seat's IK.", '1-2 h', ['people'], '205', False),
    ('234', "THE ORDER NOW (owner, 2026-11-23 night; one at a time, after each other) - OWNER 2026-11-24: first ALL THE VEHICLES (the ambulift, 238, 235, 239), then THE TERMINAL FULLY BUILT, everything that remains of it; its first job: the gate agents and THE JETWAY CORRIDOR DOORS: departures - opened early, as soon as the jetway is docked and ready and before the first passenger reaches it; arrivals - opened early too, before the first passenger off the aircraft reaches it; it STAYS OPEN and closes only when every passenger who has to go through (leaving or boarding, late ones too) has gone through; never shut in anyone's face, never open onto an undocked jetway. THEN THE FIRST RELEASE (owner, 2026-11-24): when the vehicles and the entire terminal with the jetway doors are done, build and publish everything that is ready, so the owner can test it even if the usage limit comes early; unfinished things stay switched off in it. After it: remote stands, A320 cabin, Floor 2 life, and their own release. (1) the ground crew, complete (206, then 212's apron pieces), with 235, 238 (the player operates every vehicle feature) and 239 (the new taxi bus); (2) THE EAST WING completed (213: 2b gate 13 [DONE 2026-11-24, commit 6cd2842d: the swing lobby, arrivals ways down at gates 13-16 and their closed corridor imported, wired into the layout, doors cut into the pier and passport-hall walls, checked clean], part 3 the ground-floor rooms and fittings [step 1 DONE 2026-11-24, commit 5adfbe04: the ground-floor rooms' partitions already existed in the shell, so this step added floor finishes, skirting and door linings in the 13 enclosed rooms, wired in and checked; step 2 DONE 2026-11-24, commit 648a2d04: door leaves, furniture, glowing name signs over every doorway and lamp housings added to the 104 ceiling lamps, wired in and checked clean (one unidentified flicker left at 0.0009 sq m, judged negligible; the trolley store has empty bays - no luggage-trolley prop exists in the project yet, a small 3D job still to do)], part 4 escape stairs [DONE 2026-11-24, commit d38e3540: 11 closed, fire-rated emergency stairs at the stair boxes already left open in the shell (sheet 9c), each with a door from the Floor 2 walkway, a door to its ground-floor hall and its own alarmed door onto the apron; wired in and checked clean (no flicker or cut on any new piece; still to do: stair 21 at the concourse's east end - sheet 9 gives no outdoor ground level or exterior details for it yet); the doors stay shut (alarm-only, no passenger use; part 5 opens what needs opening)], part 5 connections and swing gates [DONE 2026-11-24, commit 9683a107: the 13 closed passport-control glass leaves (booths and e-gates) and the transfer-lane exit onto the walkway became real hinged doors, shut by default, wired in and checked clean (flicker unchanged, two new cuts on the transfer door against the walkway wall, most likely already removed there by the game's touch-box, same pattern already seen on that wall; routes clear); still to do: the gate 13 two-case swing-direction logic and locking the passport-wall passenger doors both need C++ and belong with to-do 212/214]) and THE WEST WING completed (213: part 1 the claim hall and arrivals hall fit-out [DONE 2026-11-24, commit bb9c23b5: the already-built shell got its 8 missing pieces - an oversize baggage belt, lost and found, claim hall toilets (women's, men's, accessible), arrivals duty free, a real small cafe replacing the placeholder counter, an info desk, 2 ATMs and 2 SIM kiosks, reusing existing fixture parts where they already existed; wired in and checked (flicker 22 vs 17 for the wing alone, the +5 being self-flicker inside reused tax-free pieces, not newly introduced; cuts unchanged at 56; 3 route hits, all pre-existing, one a real pre-existing clash where a desk panel blocks an office door at x -70, unrelated to this fit-out; no game walkthrough done yet], part 2 taxi ranks and coach bays [DONE 2026-11-24, commit 7f2a0736: taxi rank west (10 cabs, x -75..-5) and east (11 cabs, x 158..238) painted into the forecourt kerb lane with a new taxi shelter each (x -40 and 198), and 5 new coach bays by the coach station (13 x 3.3 m, x 472..549); the pavement and kerb lane east of x 241 were already lengthened to x 552 by East Wing part 1 (commit 554b1504); pay machines left out, as the design sheet itself says they only make sense once the car park gets a vehicle exit (sheet 2f, still waiting on the owner's OK); road checker run clean, the only 2 road failures (TOWN-MAIN-STREET-10, TOWN-TORGVEGUR-1) pre-existing and unrelated; not yet seen in the game, and the walking map still needs a re-bake], part 3 the Grand Hall's own kiosks, bag drop, service desk, boarding-pass gates [DONE 2026-11-24, commits cd45bee1 and 98b36d2d: 2 rows of 6 self-service check-in kiosks, a new airline service/ticket desk, boarding-pass e-gates with reader lanes at the 2 security doors, 2 trolley bays, and 6 new self-service bag-drop machines at island 2's east end (replacing 6 of the area's 8 old placeholder points; 2 kept as spare capacity); wired in and checked clean on every new piece (0 new flicker, 0 new cuts, routes clear); two things still open, both pre-existing/behavioural, not fit-out defects: column C-6 stands in bag-drop machine 6's queue space exactly as the sheet puts it, and passengers are not yet routed to use the bag-drop machines (PaxFlow doesn't send them there yet); not yet seen in the game]); (3) THE CURVED ROOF: look at it too, not only the curved gate windows - check the whole wave roof against its approved sheets and finish what is missing; (4) THE REMOTE STANDS R0-R7 with the bus road, depot, bus lounge and apron bus; (5) the A320 cabin (209) with the APU sound (237); (6) Floor 2 life (215). The missing airport vehicles (236) come after the release. AFTER ALL OF THIS, with the terminal built according to the latest blueprints: build the game and PUBLISH EVERYTHING TOGETHER (owner, 2026-11-24). The texture pass (235) comes right after the crew. The full test comes after the wings, on the owner's word. ANY CHANGE MADE TO THE TERMINAL FROM NOW ON GOES INTO THE BUILD BEFORE PUBLISHING (owner, 2026-11-24): the east wing (2b, gate 13, parts 3/4/5), the west wing and the Grand Hall fittings (213), the curved roof, and the curved gate windows (built 2026-11-23 for 10 gates) carried to every new gate the wings add.", '', ['none'], '206, 213', False),
    ('235', "AFTER THE CREW (owner, 2026-11-24) - LIFELIKE TEXTURES ON EVERY APRON VEHICLE (cleaning van, fuel, catering, water, toilet, stairs, de-icing, ambulift): no plain flat white, red or yellow; real paint, plastic, rubber and metal, and lifelike lens and reflector detail on headlights, taillights and all other lights. Also: the dash must not look like glossy wood, live instruments instead of blank screens, no body colour inside the van's window frames, check the truck footwells for holes, a cab of its own for the catering truck. Seen on the ambulift (check every vehicle): the headlamps' cover glass is so dark and mirroring that the lamps behind it vanish, six lamp stems touch their lenses (also the de-icer?), the paint's grain is far too coarse close up. No renders to send. Every vehicle can be sat in and driven by the player and by the NPCs. The ambulift and the later ambulance (231) flash blue. FUEL TRUCK model parts still to add in this pass, then their player control: tank fill coupling, fill lever, charge flap, ticket button, manhole lids as own parts with a way up (the lids are one mesh now). FUEL TRUCK: FLOW label belongs not on the work-light button. WATER TRUCK model parts to split off, then their player control: gauges, LCD, panel buttons, fill cap, glove box, manholes, charge flap. TOILET TRUCK parts to split off or add: rinse fill cap, gauges, sinking buttons, coupling cam levers, charge flap, manhole, gloves and visor. SHARED CAB PARTS, all vehicles, to split off or add, then their player control: gear and park-brake levers, stalks, switches for lights, beacon and hazard, the van's park brake, sun visors, glove boxes, heater knobs, window switches. Each vehicle its own horn (now all the tractor's) and real light beams from headlamps and work lights. CATERING TRUCK parts to split off or add, then their control: chocks, cones, interior light, straps and nets, sinking tail-lift buttons, galley boxes and trolley doors that open. PASSENGER STAIRS (both): chocks, cones, hand pump, lockers, side flaps, lower gate, canopy flaps, light switch and ground controls missing; the stop buttons are merged (cannot sink); the platform buttons have no labels. DE-ICER: panel and cabin buttons and both stops merged (cannot sink); cab stop, outriggers, tank levels and a real cabin horn button missing; the nozzle hose reel does not turn; coupling caps fixed. BELT LOADER (parts still missing): light and indicator stalks, a belt-speed control, lettering on the panel buttons, buttons, emergency stops, direction lever, dash switch and key that move; a front bumper that yields. BAGGAGE TRACTOR (parts still missing): park brake, gear selector, horn, stalks, exact grip points round the wheel. PUSHBACK TUG (2026-11-24): the model lacks the wiper, horn button, indicator stalks, the bypass pin and the headset as own parts (poses only today), pivots for wheel and pedals; nose gear spots measured instead of worked out. GPU (2026-11-24): the model lacks a working selector and lamp test, contactor lamps, light switches and a cable reel. CLEANING VAN, missing parts: gear and park-brake levers; light, hazard, beacon, work-lamp and horn buttons; stalks; the cart ramp; a rear grab handle. FIRE TRUCK, missing model part: the work-light lamp heads and lenses. COACH, missing model parts: no wheelchair ramp or lift; no inside door button (the inner handle stands in for it).", '', ['none'], '212', False),
    ('236', "AFTER THE REMOTE STANDS (owner, 2026-11-24: yes) - THE MISSING AIRPORT VEHICLES, each with blueprint, lifelike model, driving, crew and hiring: snow plough and runway sweeper (first: winter in Switzerland), follow-me / airfield car, police / security car, cargo loader with container dollies, air start and air-conditioning units, aircraft maintenance van. (The ambulance belongs to 231, blue lights.) THE SNOW PLOUGH AT WORK (owner, 2026-11-24): called only when needed by real-life standards - the runway is inspected (runway condition report, code 6 to 0 for each third; loose snow over 3 mm, slush, ice or frost on it), never by a timer; it goes again only when that says so. Ahead of time and never near an aircraft: it waits at the holding point, the tower gives it a gap between flights and closes the runway, arrivals and departures hold or wait, it reports clear of the runway before anything lands or takes off. Really moves the snow: the plough blade lifts the snow off the paving in its own lane width, a bow wave of snow rolls ahead of the blade and is thrown off to the side, a cloud of powder flies from the brush and blower behind, the bank grows at the runway's edge; the cleared lane shows behind it lane by lane, lanes overlapped, then taxiways and apron. Then it sprays against ice with potassium formate (decided by the owner 2026-11-24; never salt): the sprayed strip is wet, ice melts, it keeps ice away for a real time by temperature and what falls, then the surface is inspected again. Snow stays away until it really falls again. Crew drive it, the player can too, every part with IK; lights, beacons, sound.", '', ['none'], '234', False),
    ('237', "WITH THE A320 CABIN (owner, 2026-11-24) - THE APU, HEARD, ON EVERY AIRCRAFT: real recordings (Freesound CC0), in the real order and at the real pace. Start: intake flap opens, starter whine, spool-up over about a minute, steady run; then a louder note when the bleed air / air conditioning comes on; stop: bleed off, cool-down run, spool-down, flap closes (animated). When: started on the taxi-in where no ground power waits, off once ground power is plugged in; on again about 10 min before off-blocks (the turn-round plan's own apu step), feeds the engine start, off after both engines run. Heat haze, ground power and cockpit/cabin lights follow the same state. Outside: from the tail cone, by distance and direction, muffled behind walls, jetway and glass, like every sound in the game. In the cabin: its own inside sound (low hum and air-conditioning rush, stronger at the rear, louder with a door open), also when the packs switch on. The A320's other distinct sound goes in with it: the PTU 'barking' under the floor while one engine is being started / stopped.", '', ['none'], '209', False),
    ('238', "WITH 235 (owner, 2026-11-24) - THE PLAYER OPERATES EVERY FEATURE OF EVERY AIRPORT VEHICLE, not only drives it. E (X on the pad) on the real lever, button, panel or hose in the vehicle or on its outside panel; the hand goes to it (IK), the part moves at its real speed, the same parts and the same limits the crew uses (never into an aircraft, a jetway or a person; stabilisers down before a lift goes up). Passenger stairs, open and roofed: raise and lower the stairs, jacks, canopy, lights. De-icer: boom and cabin up, down, out and round, nozzle aim, type I and type IV spray, heater, side pump panel, hand hose. Fuel truck: hose, couple, pump, fuel panel, dead-man handle. Catering truck: body lift, front platform, doors, carts. Water and toilet trucks: hoses, couplings, valves, pump, flush. Ambulift: body lift, platform, tail lift, doors, wheelchair, blue lights. Cargo loader, belt loader and tug / dollies: platforms, belts, hitches, pins. Ground power: cable, plug, start, stop. Cleaning van: doors, bags. Pushback tug: cradle or towbar, push and pull. Fire truck: monitors, hoses, lockers, lights, siren (what is there stays). Coach and the later apron bus: doors, ramp, kneel. On all of them: lights, indicators, beacons, horn, wipers, doors, windows, seat. A crew member using it at that moment keeps it (like a taken seat); the player taking over a job counts for the turn-round (the aircraft really is fuelled, de-iced, loaded). Every vehicle from now on gets this when it is built. WITH THE DE-ICER'S CONTROLS (owner, 2026-11-24): it is called only when the weather demands it, by real-life standards: plain rain above 0 C alone no longer calls it (only freezing rain or drizzle, snow, ice pellets, freezing fog, frost, snow or ice lying, or rain on a cold-soaked wing); the aircraft is looked over before departure in icing weather and the truck is asked for only if frost, ice, snow or slush is on it or falling; no holdover for active frost below -10 C (checked again before departure); it stays in its box otherwise. DE-ICER CHECK LATER: compare the holdover minutes with the FAA 2026-27 tables (the site refuses automatic reading). BAGGAGE TRACTOR, still open: arriving bags from the belt loader's foot by hand onto your own train; a left train whose bags have no flight tag is never fetched; loose carts are pushed back straight and only within 2 m; a train whose flight has left loses its bags. GPU DONE (2026-11-24, GpuYou.cpp, not run). Open: chocks and cones by hand (last job of 238); a crew tractor may tow a GPU whose panel you work; holding the plug without moving never times out; tug straightening has no collision check; no getting back into the tug while it waits for a driver mid-push. CLEANING VAN DONE (2026-11-24, GSECleanYou.cpp, not run). Open: the cart down the ramp by two people (prompt only); the cabin rows stay the cleaners' until 209; the vacuum cannot be put down. CARGO LOADER: no vehicle exists yet, so it is built under 244 / 236 with its controls at once. FIRE TRUCK DONE (2026-11-24, FireTruckYou.cpp, not run): cab switches, both stalks, wipers, work lights, both turrets by joystick, water/foam tanks that run down and refill at the station. Open: the wipers do not clear rain yet; nobody is hired to work the turrets. COACH DONE (2026-11-24, Coaches.cpp HandSpot/ReachTick/ReachEnd, not run): the hand really reaches the door button or bay handle before it moves (0.9 s round trip), cancels cleanly if you walk off. Stress-walk testing issue: a quick walk-away within 0.88 m right after E may log 'used' while the door stays shut (not run). Left for 238: shared lights check across all vehicles (236/235: all use the tractor's horn, no real light beams), chocks and cones by hand - the last job. LIGHTS DONE (2026-11-24, commit e21c0d7a, not run): AGSE::BuildBeams gives baggage tractors, belt loaders and all 8 service vehicles real headlight/reverse/work/beacon/blue lights alongside the lens glow (dipped 3200 lm 55 m, main 9000 lm 160 m, reverse 700 lm, beacon/blue point lights ~8 m, no shadows). Horn: 8 service vehicles now share a diesel horn (BLHorn) instead of the baggage tractor's; still shared, not per-kind. Left for 238: chocks and cones by hand - the last job. CATERING CHOCKS/CONES, model only (2026-11-24, commit 26f6f426, not run): own Chock_0/1 in a tray, two nesting Cone_0/1 on a post, IK hand/place points, imported and checked (no clash, no flicker). Not yet wired to hand controls. Left for 238: the pick/place code for the catering truck's chocks and cones, then the same for passenger stairs (model, then code) - the last job. CATERING CHOCKS/CONES CODE DONE (2026-11-24, commit 086b02c3, not run): catering truck's own chocks and cones now have hand controls for crew and player, same pattern as the refueller. Crew driver sets both chocks and cones out before legs go down, stows them after. Player: 8 take/put controls, legs need both chocks set, top cone off first/on last, 30m leash while carrying. Not verified: poses, hand grips, walk route, hand-typed collision boxes for set-out cones. Left for 238: passenger stairs chocks/cones, model then code - the last job. PASSENGER STAIRS CHOCKS/CONES, model only (2026-11-24, commit 10d88628, not run): both open and roofed stairs get own Chock_0/1 in a ChockTray (left side box, set at the left front wheel, not the drive wheel - the mud flap is in the way there) and two nesting Cone_0/1 on a post in a ConeTray (front deck, reach over the right front fender), same point names as the catering truck plus pivots. 0 flicker pairs, no clash, imported. Not wired to hand controls. Left for 238: the pick/place code for the passenger stairs' chocks and cones - the last job. PASSENGER STAIRS CHOCKS/CONES CODE DONE (2026-11-24, commit 2e5bcdae, not run): hand controls for both open and roofed stairs, same system as the catering truck, for crew and player. Not verified: crew walking routes and hand grips; cone 0's set-out spot may land under a fuselage; a stairs job with no helper waits forever for chocks (warning every 4 min). TO-DO 238 IS NOW FULLY DONE - chocks and cones by hand, the last job, closed on all vehicles.", '', ['none'], '235', False),
    ('239', "AFTER 238, BEFORE THE WINGS (owner, 2026-11-24) - THE NEW TAXI BUS: an exact COPY of the yellow van's 3D file (Art/CleaningVan - the owner calls it the ambulance; its file stays untouched), named TaxiBus; it replaces the present taxi bus (that file is kept under another name, not used). Same body. Off: the roof light, every sticker, stripe and plate, the cleaning kit and shelves inside. On: a lit taxi sign on the roof, passenger seats as high quality as the coach's own seats (owner: the same quality; start from Art/CoachSeat, sized for the van) with belts (driver + up to 8), side windows along the passenger room, the sliding door with step and grab handle, lights inside, taximeter, luggage space at the back doors, a livery of a made-up taxi-bus company. IK: passengers walk to it, the sliding door opens, they step in one after another, take their own seat and sit (the coach riders' system, VehicleRiders), out again at the other end; the driver with hands on the wheel; the player can drive it, ride in any seat and work everything on it (238). Waits at the landside kerb. 4K photos once modelled. MODEL DONE (2026-11-24, commit 8523ed5c, not imported): cleaning van body+chassis+wheels, roof taxi sign, taximeter, 9 coach-built seats with belts, 2 side windows/side, sliding-door grab bar, luggage floor, plain livery. Old taxi bus kept under another name, not used (commit 84070100, Art/TaxiBusOld). Not run: import (timed out), controls (VehicleRiders seating, sliding door, player drive, 238-style controls).", '', ['none'], '238', False),
    ('239b', "TAXI BUS, REMAINING GAPS (2026-11-24, after 239's import+controls, commit efd63109, and riders file, commit fac26109): a hired driver needs a 'taxi' role in Sim/ADPCrew.cpp and a TaxiDriver duty/task in StaffPeople.h/.cpp (none exist yet, so HiresRole('taxi') is always false). Passengers at the kerb are Mass entities (TerminalCrowd), not actors, and PaxFlow has no landside taxi demand or hand-over to UVehicleRiders yet (that system only fits coaches today). Belts need their own mesh step in Art/TaxiBus/make_taxibus.py (only the IK_Buckle_*/IK_BeltTongue_* points exist, no belt/buckle meshes). The taximeter and roof sign need a fare state and a TaxiTick hook in GSE.h/GSE.cpp (AGSE::TaxiControls is private, no hook today); the meter has no mesh part, the sign has lens parts (TaxiSign_F/B_Lens) ready. Build one piece at a time: (1) belt/buckle/meter meshes [DONE 2026-11-24, commit 34fc79a5]. (2) TaxiDriver hire role [DONE 2026-11-24, commit 39543de9, re-imported: taxi bus own re-import also done this commit] - hired driver never takes the wheel yet: every staff member appears at an airside ramp room, no landside route to the kerb exists, and ADPStaff.cpp has no wage line for 'taxi' (uses the general wage). [DONE 2026-11-24, commit 907fafeb, pushed]: the candidate point x 156, z 46 was checked and is NOT outside (it opens into F1-EAST-LANE inside the East Hall - the terminal-plan.json \"OUTSIDE\" label there is wrong), so it was not used. Built instead: the driver spawns in the car park at (46.25, -58.5) only when the player cannot see the spot, crosses the zebra at x 48 (same crossing points as passengers), walks the pavement at z -19.5 to a place behind the bus facing the kerb, and a new AGSE::TaxiTick hook (GSE.h/GSE.cpp, body in GSEService.cpp) walks them round to the road side and climbs them into the driver seat, hands on the wheel; at shift end they climb out, walk back, and go home the same way. The 'taxi' wage line (600, same as bus) was added in ADPStaff.cpp. Not checked: street furniture on the pavement between x -53 and 48; whether the player can still take the wheel while the hired driver is seated. Note for piece 3: AGSE::TaxiTick as built only handles the driver's walk/seat - it does NOT yet do the fare state or light the meter/sign; piece 3 still needs its own fare-state logic, likely added to this same hook rather than a second one. (3) TaxiTick meter/sign hook [DONE 2026-11-24, commit 1228dec0]: AGSE::TaxiTick also now owns the fare (on/off, distance, time, waiting time in GSE.h); roof sign lenses glow amber on duty + free, dark during a fare; meter digit window glows red during a fare; the meter button (driver's seat) starts/ends a fare with a placeholder tariff (CHF 6.50 start, 3.50/km, 1.00/min under 15 km/h), prompt shows CHF/km/minutes. Public TaxiFare(bool, Who) is the stub for piece 4 to call - only the button can set a fare today, no passenger trigger yet. (4) PaxFlow landside hand-over [HELD, owner 2026-11-24]: held until after the East/West terminal wings (to-do 234) are built, including the real West wing taxi rank (to-do 213) - building this now would hook into today's placeholder kerb layout and routes, which the finished terminal will likely replace. Same reasoning as to-do 202 (full 3D boarding), already sequenced after the terminal; this joins it.", '', ['none'], '239', False),

    ('240', "Passenger stairs: both seats come through the cab's back wall (owner, 2026-11-24, photo docs/Pictures/Vehicles/Issues/240-stairs-seats-through-wall.jpg): the two seat backs and the headrest stick out of the cab's rear panel. Move the seats forward or the wall back so nothing passes through, keep the sitting place, legroom and reach to the wheel right; same fix on the roofed stairs. Only the stairs: no other vehicle is checked unless the owner names it. Done in the model and imported, not seen in the game yet.", '', ['none'], '238', True),
    ('241', "LATER TODAY, AFTER 238 (owner, 2026-11-24) - IK THAT IS STILL MISSING ON THE VEHICLES: (1) seat belts put on and taken off by everyone in every vehicle (points exist); (2) ambulift: wheelchairs pushed by the player, chair brakes, the stretcher, straps and belts; (3) toilet truck: carrying with both hands; (4) coach passengers' hands that end 2 - 15 cm short (fix made, not run); (5) passengers and cabin crew walking the mobile stairs, hands on the rails (needs the A320's doors and cabin, 209); (6) the taxi bus with 239. Catering: second hand on the trolley's handle; loose trolleys left behind jump home after 120 s and every trolley vanishes at the aircraft's sill (no teleporting: somebody brings them back, the trolley goes inside). Passenger stairs: the helper who takes over could meet the player stepping back onto the stairs. DE-ICER: the look-over for ice is a two-minute task of the flight, nobody is seen doing it: a ground crew member walks round the aircraft. BELT LOADER: carrying a bag off the belt, the bag at the top goes back up after 10 s without being seen.", '', ['none'], '238', False),
    ('242', "JUST PRIOR TO PUBLISHING: after the terminal, right before the first release (owner, 2026-11-24) - AIRCRAFT HOLD SHORT OF THE RUNWAY AND AT TAXIWAY CROSSINGS, WITH THE REAL MARGINS. RUNWAY (ICAO Doc 4444): (1) nobody is held closer to the runway in use than the runway-holding position (7.6.3.1.3); the game's is 107.5 m from the centre line (the code F distance, Annex 14; 90 m is the code 4 precision value: check against the painted line); an aircraft coming off counts as clear only when ALL of it, tail included, is beyond that line (7.6.3.1.2.2). (2) No take-off until the departure before has crossed the runway end or started a turn, or every lander before is clear of the runway (7.9.2). (3) No landing over the threshold until the same is true (7.10.1), or reduced (7.11.7): the one before is airborne and 2400 m past the threshold, only with 5 km visibility, cloud base 300 m, at most 5 kt tailwind; never for a departure behind a lander (7.11.5). If it is not clear: GO-AROUND. (4) Nobody lines up in front of a landing aircraft: only once it has passed the holding point (7.6.3.1.3.2). (5) Wake turbulence, same runway: light or medium behind heavy 2 min, light behind medium 2 min, medium behind super 3 min; from an intersection 3 / 3 / 4 min (5.8.3). (6) A line-up only if the departure can be 2400 m away or past the end before the next arrival is at the threshold: worked out from its speed and distance, no fixed time. CROSSINGS: (7) hold behind the intermediate holding position: distance of the crossing centre line by the code letter of who uses it (C 26 m = A320, D 37, E 43.5, F 51; EASA CS-ADR-DSN Table D-1, taxiway centre line to object), or 60 m where no line is painted (no-marking sign distance, CS ADR-DSN); (8) who goes first: an aircraft leaving the runway, then the one from the right (Annex 2 rules of the air, from knowledge: check); nobody enters a crossing it cannot clear; head-on on one taxiway never happens (the later one waits before the last junction); (9) in a queue: nose to tail gap so the jet blast is clear (about 50 m, not a published figure: check). Vehicles give way to all of it. TODAY IN THE CODE (SimTraffic.cpp ~421-428, 164/264, 337): a departure waits at 107.5 m while someone rolls, rolls out or is below 200 m on approach (about 3.8 km), then 20 s; MISSING: a climbing aircraft not yet past the end is ignored, no wake times, an arrival never waits for or goes around because of an aircraft on the runway, an aircraft leaving is clear as soon as it starts taxiing (its tail still over the line), two departures in one queue stand in the same spot, no crossing check between two taxiing aircraft, taxi in and out share A and T head-on. The tower's words follow the same state. HEAD-ON FIX (from the layout, no new pavement): a one-way circle that turns with the runway. 08 in use: in by A2, A east, P3, T west; out by T west, P1, A west to A1 (as today). 26 in use: in by A2, A WEST, P1, T east; out by T east, P3, A east to A3 (today arrivals also come down P3, against the departures). So nobody meets on A, P1, P3 or T. Left over are the two ends of T beyond the links (stands 9-11 west of P1, 14-16 east of P3): one aircraft at a time there, reserved; the other waits on the link (168 m, room for a whole A320 behind the line) or stays at its stand. When the runway in use changes, the new direction starts only once T and the links are empty; each aircraft keeps the route it was given. P2 stays the spare way if T is blocked. Cargo side the same with K, Q0, Q2, B.", '', ['none'], '', False),
    ('243', "BEFORE PUBLISHING, with 242 (owner, 2026-11-24) - THE AIRCRAFT LIGHTS ITSELF UP AS IN REAL LIFE (A320 now; STANDARD FOR EVERY FUTURE AIRCRAFT, AI and the player's bought ones). TODAY (SimTraffic.cpp AddLights, 12 glow balls): the lamps are seen but give no light, so the aircraft is dark at night; parked aircraft have none at all. WANTED: every lamp shines on the aircraft itself, the ground and what is near it: red beacons (top, belly) sweep red over fuselage, wing roots, tail and apron; white strobes flash on the wingtip and the wing's trailing edge; nav red / green / white tint the wingtips and the tail cone; landing lights (wing root) beam onto the runway and graze nacelle and flap; nose taxi, take-off and runway turn-off lights on the nose gear, its doors, the belly and the taxiway ahead. Also there in reality: wing and engine scan lights on the leading edge and nacelle, logo lights on the fin, light from the cabin windows and cockpit (with 209 when the cabin exists). LAMPS: lens + housing instead of a ball where the model allows. USE: the real switching already in UpdateLights (strobe on the runway and in the air, beacon from pushback until the engines stop, landing lights on the runway and below 10000 ft, taxi lights moving, turn-off); parked: beacon off, nav and logo on at night, cabin windows lit. HOW: one light table per aircraft type (place, colour, angle, strength, who shines on what: Data/aircraft-lights.json or the type table) used by one function for AI, parked and the player's aircraft; lamp system, distance limits, no flares, EVERY aircraft lights ITSELF up at any distance (owner's question 2026-11-24: none may stay dark at night): the light on its own skin is worked out in the aircraft's material from the light table (lamp place, colour, angle, on/off value), no light source needed; real light sources are added only on the nearest aircraft, for the ground, people and vehicles round them; far aircraft's landing light pool on the ground is a soft light decal; fade between the two unseen (frame rate measured on the owner's word). A new aircraft type isn't finished without its table. OWNER OVERRIDE (2026-11-24): this comes before the terminal's full test too, not only before publishing - built before that test fires. OWNER (2026-11-24, added): the nose gear's taxi light goes OFF in the final turn onto the gate or remote stand - once it is lined up on the stand's heading (airfield.json) and closing in on its stop, BEFORE it stops (from its path and heading, not a timer); before that it stays on while taxiing. Every lamp that needs power goes off when the aircraft is shut down (cold and dark) and comes back when it is powered again. BUILT 2026-11-24 (step 1, compile only, not seen in a game run): Data/aircraft-lights.json (A320 table, 20 lamps, groups beacon/strobe/nav/logo/landing/takeoff/taxi/turnoff/wing-scan/engine-scan/cabin-window) read by one component (Source AircraftLights.h/.cpp, UADPAircraftLights::Fit) on every AI and parked aircraft; SimTraffic Blink keeps the real switching and hands it over (final-turn nose rule, beacon off while doors open, engines-off = parked state); parked: beacon off, nav + logo at night while powered, powered only during the turnaround's work (just in, short turns, 75 min before off-blocks), else cold and dark. Light on the skin and round it: one light decal per lamp (M_AircraftLampLight, Tools/make_aircraft_lamp_light.py) - a decal, not the A320's own material slots (Nanite + re-imports); real spot lights (no shadows, no flares) on the 2 nearest aircraft within 300 m, faded against the beam's ground decal over 2.5 s. LEFT: lens + housing + reflector lamp models on the A320 (still glow balls - a 3D step); cabin windows (no lamps until 209's cabin is in); the player's own aircraft (no 3D one exists yet - it calls Fit when it does); new lamp places (tail strobe, logo, wing/engine scan) estimated, not measured on the model; frame rate not measured.", '', ['none'], '', False),
    ('244', "BEFORE PUBLISHING, after the terminal and before 242 / 243 (owner, 2026-11-24: the crew fully in game before publishing) - THE WHOLE CREW IN 3D. Checked in the code 2026-11-24: 23 jobs can be hired, in 5 of them the hired people do the work in 3D (ground crew, fuel, catering, cleaning, water and toilet; compiled, not run). STILL TO BUILD, in this order: (1) the de-icing operator you hire (the truck uses ground crew today); (2) gate agents with the jetway doors (already the first terminal job) [DOOR STAFFING DONE 2026-11-24, commit 07feec54: the jetway corridor door now only opens on its own when a gate agent is on shift (PaxFlow.h/.cpp, same pattern as check-in/security staffing), warns + tells the owner to hire one if not; compiled, not run. Still missing: an actual 3D gate-agent person at the door - this only wires the hiring role to the door's staffing check]; (3) check-in agents at the desks with the passport hand-over (to-do 210, today only a head count opens the desk); (4) security officers at the lanes (today only a head count); (5) captain, first officer and cabin crew: through the terminal and the jetway, aboard before the passengers, off after them (to-do 202); (6) mechanics: the walk-round look at the aircraft; (7) firefighters living in the station and turning out with the trucks; (8) coach drivers; (9) the tower and the operations room; (10) one shared desk-job system for shops, lounge, baggage claim and car park; people stand behind the counter in every shop, the lounge bar and the cafes (as many as its counter has room for - most shops fit more than one), a passenger or crew member who bought something walks up and pays there (cash or an electronic card machine on the counter, real tap/insert motion); THE BAR'S DRINKING (owner, 2026-11-24): too many drinks at a bar makes that person drunk for 20 seconds (stumbling walk, blurred/swaying camera if it's you), remade from the Babylon edition's lounge-drunk.ts with Unreal's own data and effects, not ported code; (11) a face is never seen twice after a hire in a talk, pictures of the candidates on the Crew screen. ALSO BEFORE THE FIRST RELEASE (owner, 2026-11-24: all crew before publish), with what they need built first: (12) marshaller; (13) the remote stands with the bus road, the apron bus and its driver; (14) the cargo loader with dollies (from 236) and its crew; (15) cabin crew at work inside the aircraft, needs the A320 cabin (209, the owner's OK on its pictures). NOTHING HOLDS THEM BACK (owner, 2026-11-24): each job is followed from coming on shift to going home: a free way on foot and by vehicle, doors they may open, their vehicle and place not blocked, hands reach, two crews never wait for each other for ever, shift change, breaks, save and load in the middle of a job; whoever still can't go on gets loose by themselves or says why in the game (never stuck in silence, never teleported). Waiting that is real stays: too few people on shift, an aircraft not there yet, weather, a lane or door in use. All 23 jobs work in 3D before publishing. THE CREW TEST (owner, 2026-11-24: yes, before publishing, once the crew is complete): the same way as the career chain (Tools/new_game_test.sh --career --from N, ChainTest.cpp): one short stage per job, a save point at the start of each, and after a fault the run goes on from that stage; stages that passed are run again only if files they use were changed. Faults are fixed together, then one compile. POLICE (owner, 2026-11-24, as in to-do 69f): not a shift job - they are the call that already comes for a violent passenger, built with their own hired 3D person (not the security officer's stand-in) once crew work is done, same escort/cuffs behaviour. Uniforms: the owner said clothes come later; asked again  OWNER OVERRIDE (2026-11-24): the full crew (this to-do) can now wait until after publishing - no longer required before the first release. Whatever remains is built after publish only if enough usage is left, and only when the owner says so - never started on its own.2026-11-24.", '', ['none'], '', False),
    ('245', "LATER (owner, 2026-11-24: not now, after 239b) - THE TAXI BUS'S FADE OUT AND BACK IN, DONE PROPERLY. Today's piece 4 (239b) just has a taxi vanish after a short timer standing in for its off-screen trip, then sit idle at the rank until needed again - good enough for now. Wanted later: (1) it only fades out once it is genuinely far from the action, never a visible pop near the player; (2) once faded out it does NOT just reappear idle a moment later - it only fades back in when it is actually needed, either sent to pick someone up at the terminal kerb, or arriving already carrying a passenger from wherever it was 'going' (so some taxis can appear already occupied, as if completing a different incoming trip); (3) the fade-in is just as far from the action as the fade-out, same rule both ways; (4) because faded-out taxis are invisible, there must still always be a believable number of taxis visibly present/circulating - spawn extra taxis in as needed to keep that up, using the same road and behaviour as the real ones. Keep it to the taxi bus only; no new off-map destination or visible drop-off scene needed for this, just the fade timing and on-demand respawn/pool logic.", '', ['none'], '239b', False),
    ('246', "AT THE NEXT PUBLISH, AND EVERY ONE AFTER (owner, 2026-11-25; not for 0.18.2) - THE GAME HE STARTS IS ALWAYS THE NEWEST ONE: after 0.18.2 his desktop icon still opened 0.18.1 (F:/Airport Dynasty Pro Unreal) and he ran 0.18.0 from F:/ADP-Packaged by mistake. At every publish: (1) the installed game behind the desktop icon is replaced by the new version, the World Terrain folder, careers and settings kept; (2) the desktop and Start menu icons, and any other shortcut to the game, open that newest version; (3) old versions in F:/ADP-Packaged can't be started by mistake; (4) Tools/release_pc.sh does it by itself and reads the version inside the exe behind every icon - the publish is not done until they all say the new version; (5) whenever the AI starts the game for him or for a test of the release, it is the newest version. Never while the game is running.", '', ['none'], '-', False),
    ('247', "OWNER, 2026-11-25 (seen in 0.18.2; not built yet) - ESC, THEN STAY, MUST NOT LOAD THE AIRPORT AGAIN: Esc in the 3D world only opens a question; Stay (and Esc again, a click beside it, Save now + Save) just takes it away and the game world is there at once, no loading screen. Cause, read in the code: AADPHUD::OnEscape puts the whole menus up (ToggleGame) so the page can ask (__ueLeave3D, Airline-Manager-Pro-Babylon/src/main.ts); every answer that stays calls back3d, and the tick's rule 'the menus were up and now are not' (ADPHUD.cpp, StartLoading) starts the loading screen as if you came from the title. Nothing is unloaded meanwhile. To do: the question as a native C++ panel over the living 3D world (no web layer), same four choices and Esc closing it; the loading only after a real Leave.", '', ['none'], '-', False),
    ('248', "OWNER, 2026-11-25 (seen in 0.18.2; not built yet) - THE ROADS BEHIND THE TERMINAL: that entire area is missing its textures. First find out which site this was on (the owner plays from a plain start) and whether it is the materials, the late-built world (roads, town, land come after the loading, ADPLateWorld) or the distance; then the whole area checked, not just one road. The roads, the walkways and the crossings, fixed before the one performance run (owner, 2026-11-25). Belongs with 147 (zebra crossings, and the road textures at night).", '', ['none'], '-', False),
    ('249', "OWNER, 2026-11-25 (seen in 0.18.2; not built yet) - AN INVISIBLE WALL BETWEEN THE ROAD AND THE PAVEMENT OUTSIDE THE MAIN HALL: walking from the road system over to the pavement, the owner had to jump over it. It is before the main hall, not the hall itself. Find which collision stands there (the kerb, the pavement's edge, the ground's own collision against the road, or a leftover box) with the collision view, and walk the whole length of that edge, both directions, also as a passenger and with a trolley; a kerb is stepped up without jumping.", '', ['none'], '-', False),
    ('250', "OWNER, 2026-11-25 (seen in 0.18.2; not built yet) - THE TAXI BUS'S DRIVER'S SEAT (the taxi bus, not the airport's coach): looking around, your own body glitches through the view. ONLY THE FIRST TIME you got in (OWNER the same day) - later times were fine, so it is something of the first boarding rather than the seat or the pose. Cause not found. Look first at: the head is hidden only once, on the change (bHide != bHead in AServiceDrive::View), so any part of your body that is made, dressed, loaded or shown again after that moment (the borrowed body, its clothes, hair, the eye body in AADPWalker::ShowHead / UseEyeBody / LendBody) stays in the picture until you get in again; add logging of what is visible near the camera on the first boarding after a fresh start. Less likely now: AServiceDrive::View (ServiceDrive.cpp) puts the camera at the rig's eyes (Rig.Eyes) and turns it there while the body stays facing ahead, and only the head is hidden, so the neck, collar, shoulders or clothes can come into the picture; and whether the clothed body really sits where the rig does in this seat (PIV/IK points in Data/taxibus-riders.json). To check after a fresh start, the first boarding and the second: looking fully left, right, up and down, parked and steering, every outfit, both bodies; the same code carries the other service vehicles, so their driver's and passenger's seats too.", '', ['none'], '-', False),
    ('251', "OWNER, 2026-11-25 (not built yet) - WAYPOINT MODE ON THE MAP: waypoints can only be made while waypoint mode is on, and it is on only after clicking its button. The button also stands in the map view, right next to the legend (the set-waypoint button the owner means, and everywhere else it stands: the same one). Its words: WAYPOINT MODE while the mode is off, EXIT WAYPOINT MODE while it is on (not 'start waypoint'). Now: in the full-screen map every left click inside the building adds a waypoint and a right click on one removes it (SHoloMap::OnMouseButtonDown), no mode at all; only CLEAR WAYPOINTS is a button (ADPHUD.cpp), shown once there is a route. To do, native C++: the mode in SHoloMap, clicks only then make waypoints, the button beside the LEGEND tab drawn and clickable (its click never makes a waypoint), the same for the controller, the legend's line 'Click where you want to go' following the mode, the waypoints already made kept on leaving the mode; off again when the map is opened anew.", '', ['none'], '-', False),
    ('252', "OWNER, 2026-11-25 (not built yet; with 251, same row of buttons) - CONTROL JETWAY ON THE MAP: in the full-screen holographic map, beside the waypoint mode button and the legend, a button reading CONTROL JETWAY. Clicked, the jetway starts (drives out to the aircraft) and the button reads TURN OFF JETWAY; clicked then, the jetway goes back to its place. Reuse AJetwayRigs::Dock / Undock / State (the lower-right jetway button in ADPHUD.cpp does this already with DOCK / UNDOCK / MOVING for the nearest jetway within 40 m); native C++. Which jetway (owner, 2026-11-25): only the nearest one, the jetway that belongs to your gate; never picked on the map; its gate is shown. Known limits to solve: while it drives, Dock and Undock both refuse, so TURN OFF pressed halfway out must turn it round (or stop it) and not be ignored; no aircraft at the gate, or a type it can't dock to: the reason is shown (LastNote) and the button stays CONTROL JETWAY; never pulled back with people in it or while boarding goes on (crew and automatic docking use the same jetway: the button shows its real state). The controller works it too; its click never makes a waypoint.", '', ['none'], '-', False),
    ('253', "OWNER, 2026-11-25 (seen in 0.18.2; not built yet) - THE TABLET'S ANIMATION LAGS (only the tablet's; the message's word was 'lacking'): both when it is brought up into view, and the last part, when it is brought further up close until its screen fills most of the picture. Look into it and improve it: smooth all the way, both directions. Cause not measured. Where to look (AADPHUD::TickTablet, Devices.cpp): just as it comes up the web page draws the whole home screen as a picture and sends it over, unpacked on the game's own thread (__ueTabletHome, TabletScreen), and again every 30 s while held; the portrait blur ramps to f/1.2 over the whole picture meanwhile (the phone has that too and is fine, so less likely); it is placed from the view once per HUD tick, which can be a frame behind when you move; and at the end the 3D model is hidden in one frame and the live screen laid over (OpenDevice), whose place and size may not match. First measure the frame times of both animations (ADP_PERF_SCOPE), then fix: the picture made ahead of time or off the game thread, the last frame of the 3D tablet matching the live screen exactly.", '', ['none'], '-', False),
    ('254', "My career on the tablet and the PCs (owner, 2026-11-25): clicking My career (the app's name in the code: career) opens your career first, as now, but with the full vertical navigation of the former UI on the left side, so every single part can be reached from there, hiring staff included. My career is an entry of that vertical menu too. The former menu had: Overview, Fleet and aircraft market, Routes, Jobs, Schedule, Dispatch, Planner, Maintenance, Finance, Logbook, Pilots and staff (hiring), Ranking, Career, News, Co-op, Office, Operations, Hangars, Lounges, Airport, Services, Court and Settings (old list: NAV_ORDER in game/src/ui/shell.ts). The shortcuts on the desktop of the tablet and the PCs stay as well (Staff, rankings, markets, aircraft market and the others like them; owner, 2026-11-25): each opens its own part directly. The vertical navigation is shown on the left in every one of them, whichever shortcut you came in by, with the part you are in marked; it is not on the desktop itself. The same on the tablet and on all PCs. Every app leads to its own part (owner, 2026-11-25): the Overview icon opens the Overview, Staff the staff and hiring, Ranking the ranking, Markets the markets, Aircraft market the aircraft market, My career your career, and so on for Mail, Calendar, Bank, My Documents, Photos, Videos, Recorder, Web browser, Settings, Messages, Phone, Store and Weather; the parts belong together, so a link in one opens the right other one. Today only the Overview icon opens the former UI; the other apps open a single screen without its navigation (lapAppHtml in game/src/screens/laptop.ts), and Schedule, Dispatch and Flight planner are icons on the Operations computer only. Each entry is checked: it opens on the tablet and the PCs, it works on the real simulation, and it leads back to the menu; an entry whose screen does not exist any more is named to the owner, not left out silently. Native C++, no web layer; mouse, touch on the screen and controller", '', ['none'], '-', False),
    ('255', "The old Overview must never be seen in the game (owner, 2026-11-25: never, ever): when the tablet is closed, the old full-screen Overview glitches through for a moment. It is reachable only from the tablet and from the computers and monitors in the game. Found in the code, not proven by a run: on closing, the page takes away what hides the old interface (class ue-dev, ueDeviceClose in game/src/screens/ue-device.ts and __ueDeviceDone in src/main.ts) before Unreal has hidden the page (AADPHUD::DeviceDone in Devices.cpp), so it shows in between. Fix: Unreal hides the page first, and the old interface stays hidden always, whatever opens or closes (tablet, phone, computers, jetway screen, Esc menu, loading, the title screen's way back). Also found: the Overview opened from the tablet fills the whole game screen (AADPHUD::OverviewFrom); it belongs inside the tablet's own screen or the monitor, together with 254. The old full-screen version and every old way of entering it are removed from the game (owner, 2026-11-25): only the tablet and the PCs in the game open the Overview, on their own screens. Old ways still in the code (AADPHUD::ToggleGame): Esc with nothing open and the leave question (OnEscape, with 247), using any part whose name contains screenPanel or black (ADPHUD.cpp near line 2016), coming back from following a flight in 3D (must return to the tablet or PC it was opened from), Esc while the airport is loading, G and the pad's Y, and the page's own start on the Overview. The title screen and New Game stay. Checked by opening and closing every device and watching every frame", '', ['none'], '-', False),
    ('256', "Slow views at the gates and the apron (owner, 0.18.2): from the gates towards the jetways and to the west and east, and from the apron looking at the terminal from different views; the passenger side of the terminal runs well. Measured in the owner's own log (3840 x 1080): the graphics card is the limit, 18-25 ms usually, 27-50 ms at the bad places (drawn 20-35 fps): west gates x -213..-223, z 79-84, looking -99, -130, -148 degrees, and x -47..-52, z 69-81; a fair place is 61-65 fps. Graphics memory is nearly full (7.6 of 8.2 GB), to be checked as a cause of the stalls. Find what costs there (pass by pass), also the spikes of about 50 ms once a minute and frame generation adding no pictures at times; quality is never lowered without asking.", '', ['none'], '-', False),
    ('257', "Map shortcuts and later fast travel: a clean change of place with no stalls. Today a shortcut only moves the player; nothing at the new place is loaded first. Needed: the place, its textures and its shaders ready before it is shown, a short fade, no slow frames after; used by the holographic map shortcuts, the quick-starts and the later fast travel. Check whether the slow apron views (256) come from arriving by shortcut; walk there as the comparison.", '', ['none'], '-', False),
    ('258', "Crash on 2026-11-25 at 04:07 (0.18.2, owner's PC; crash dump exists, the log of that run is gone): read the dump, find and fix the cause.", '', ['none'], '-', False),
    ('259', "The wet apron looks too much like a mirror (owner's photo, 0.18.2, the hall shining in the whole apron at night). It was really wet: light rain in the live weather at Sion until after 04:00, dry since about 04:20, and the ground dries over 3 hours. But soaked paving gets one even shine (roughness 0.32) everywhere, even after light rain. Wanted: like real wet concrete and asphalt: darker, a broken, patchy sheen, clear reflections only in the puddles, light rain wetting it less than heavy rain, dry patches coming back first where the sun or wheels are. Check at the same time, as a suspect for 256: an even 0.32 is below the limit under which the engine traces reflections for every pixel (normally 0.4), so a wet apron may cost a lot of graphics time; measure dry against wet at the same spot.", '', ['none'], '-', False),
    ('260', "Outline glow on every interaction, everywhere in the game (owner's standard rule, 2026-11-25; first seen missing on the ground power unit's panel and buttons, 0.18.2). Today the outline leaves out doors and vehicles on purpose. Wanted: the thin outline that people have on whatever E (X on the pad) would use: every vehicle's doors, panels, buttons, switches, levers, plugs and cables, the jetway's controls, doors used by hand, and all else in the world; where parts are close together only the one you look at. Go through the list of every interaction and check each has it; later ones get it from the start. Still no outline round your own character, while you use a device or a menu, or round the vehicle's body while you sit in it.", '', ['none'], '-', False),
    ('261', "Wheels look untextured and striped, small squares on the paint (owner's photo of the pushback tug, also the ground power unit, 0.18.2). Not a missing texture: the rubber texture is there (Rubber004). The road dirt drawn over it is wrong: in set_truck_materials.py the streaks are hard 11 cm columns (VN of floor(p.x x 9), not smoothed) and the wear chips are 2.6 cm cubes with hard edges (VN(p x 38)), both fixed to the world instead of to the vehicle, so they also slide over it when it drives and wheels turn through them. It is on the fire trucks and every vehicle with M_VehicleDirt. Fix once for both materials: smooth, irregular streaks and chips from a real texture or smooth noise, in the vehicle's own space (wheels in their own), the part-washed runs (3 cm hard stripes) too. Also: the tyres are smooth with no tread; give them tread and sidewall lettering, rims with wear. Check the wheels of every vehicle.", '', ['none'], '-', False),
    ('262', "Traffic timetables always hang on a wall (owner's standard rule, 2026-11-25, photo): never on a door, a window, glass or a shutter, unless the owner says otherwise. Moved 2026-11-25: the six Floor 2 walkway boards without a wall behind them, M-F2-04 (x -201 to the north wall, x 282 to the wall at x 288), M-F2-05 (x -116 and 367 to the north wall), M-F2-06 (x 95 to the north wall at x 90), M-F2-07 (x 512 to the north wall); Tools/terminal_boards_edit.py keeps it. All other boards and every gate screen have a wall behind them (checked against the real walls). Left: see in the game that the live timetable shows on the moved ones.", '', ['none'], '-', False),
    ('263', "On-screen cursors as fast as a PC cursor (owner, 2026-11-25, photo of the jetway screen: far too slow; reported on 11-19 too). Cause in the code: the jetway screen's + (Devices.cpp 390) and the phone's pointer (Devices.cpp 241) take their movement from GetInputMouseDelta, which is already cut to 7 percent, scaled by the view's zoom (bEnableFOVScaling, so slower up close) and smoothed; the speed-up for quick movement starts at 120, which that small number hardly ever reaches, so the fix of 11-19 could not work. New: one shared function for every in-game cursor (jetway, phone, tablet, terminals, vehicle and pump panels), fed by the real mouse movement with the pointer speed and acceleration set in Windows, 1 to 1 on the screen however far the view is zoomed, no smoothing; the pad's stick quick too, with a cursor speed in Settings. Check every device, at 3840x1080 and with frame generation on and off.", '', ['none'], '-', False),
    ('264', "The TEST time and weather bar, and the map credit line, lie on top of the jetway screen while you use it (seen in the owner's photo, 2026-11-25): the bar hides for phone and tablet (HoldingDevice) but not for the jetway screen (bHmiPointer, ADPHUD.cpp ~546). Built 2026-11-25 for the jetway screen (both hidden), not run yet; the other full-size screens are still to do.", '', ['none'], '-', False),
    ('265', "BUILT 2026-11-25, not run yet (screen, camera and body ride as one with the jetway; anyone standing in a moving one who is not carried is named in the log). At one with the jetway while you control it (owner, 2026-11-25): driving it from its screen in any direction, the screen slides out of your view. Cause in the code: the close-up camera is put in front of the screen once, when you open it (OpenHmi, Devices.cpp ~336), and never moved again while the cab drives off with the screen. Fix: the camera sits on the console and moves and turns with it every frame, in the same frame as the jetway (no lag, no shake); your body stands fixed to the cab's floor at the console for as long as you control it (sideways, forward, back, up, down, cab turning, docking and undocking, 1x and faster), never sliding, falling behind or pushed into a wall, and your eyes come back to where you stand now when you close it. The same for hired gate agents at the console. CarryRiders already carries people standing in it, but only while their floor is a known jetway part: log each time someone in it isn't carried.", '', ['none'], '-', False),
    ('266', "Very bad frames outside the fire station (owner, 0.18.2, 2026-11-25). Measured in the owner's own log (3840 x 1080, 05:50 local, dark, broken clouds): standing outside at plan x -1209, z 56 the graphics card took 100-290 ms a frame (4-9 fps) in almost every direction, for the 45 s from the arrival by shortcut, also looking up (pitch 43-69) or standing still; game thread 10-29 ms, render 8-16 ms. Inside the station (x -1222, z -1) 15 ms, and in the hall with bay door 2 open (x -1211, z 19) 22 ms; gate 13 just before, 21 ms. So it is the place outdoors, not only the station's model. Find it pass by pass there (ProfileGPU, dark and day, dry and wet, and with the grass, the mast lights and their haze, the wet ground and the station's and trucks' lights each off in turn), and whether it is the graphics memory (7.6 of 8.2 GB used, 962 MB of the game in shared memory) filling up after a shortcut. Grass patches went from 7256 to 19412 far tufts there. Quality is never lowered without asking. Again at 05:52 local in fire truck 2 in front of its bay (x -1208, z 35, looking -91): a steady 88-98 ms, 9-11 fps for over a minute and no in-between pictures at all, so it repeats and is not only a moment after the shortcut; meanwhile the graphics memory was 7.6 GB with 1.2 GB pushed into the PC's shared memory (962 to 1028 MB of it the game's, still growing). Owner: inside the station, looking out through the garage doors, the frames are fine; one step outside, looking in exactly the same direction, very bad. New first suspect, before the memory, not proven: the sky with its volumetric clouds. Outside the cost mostly rises with the share of sky in the picture (median GPU: pitch 0 92 ms, 5 112, 20 150, 45 229, 65 220, 90 185; a few outliers at level pitch, 220-290), and from inside only a little sky shows through the doors. Weather BROKEN, dark (SunClock.cpp: coverage -0.05, density 0.012, the densest of the lighter themes; the clouds' own saver is on, 256 view samples, Effects.cpp 105). Measure first on the same spot: -NoClouds against clouds, CLEAR against BROKEN, night against day, view samples and the cloud render-target mode, then the list above. Owner: in daylight it is the same, outside in every direction, but looking AT the station is fine. Log in daylight (time moved by the test slider, sun 11 degrees, still BROKEN; 04:54:45-04:55:32 UTC, standing still outside at x -1204, z 33): 117-126 ms at pitch 2-3, 140-150 at pitch 8, 150-162 at pitch 13-20 looking the same way, about 190 at yaw -47 to -90, 135-146 to the other side (yaw -110 to -130), only one 20 ms frame looking down (pitch -10); no slow frames logged with the station filling the picture. 7 fps over 30 s, graphics card 144 ms, game thread 18-20 ms. So not the dark, the lamps or the station itself; what is left is the open view (sky and clouds first, the far ground, mountains and grass second). Owner: the same with hardly any clouds. Corrected 2026-11-25: the live weather takes the test bar's choice back once a minute (CLEAR 04:56:34, BROKEN again 04:57:03; CLEAR 04:58:10-04:59:03), so the 230-257 ms first read as CLEAR were BROKEN again; in the first CLEAR half minute the frames were still bad by the owner's word and the 30 s average (115 ms). In the second, standing still at x -1197, z 52, yaw 13: 154 ms until the click, 68 ms half a second later and 23 ms on average from then on. But BROKEN again (dark at 04:59:03, day from 04:59:09) stayed good at 37 ms until 04:59:39, when on the same spot and weather it jumped from 29 to 148 ms within one second while turning (yaw 47 to 15), and then stayed at 130-137 ms walking 100 m. So neither the weather nor the direction alone decides: the graphics card SWITCHES between a good state and a bad one, about 4-6 times as slow (owner: 5-6 frames, then suddenly 60-70). 05:00-05:02 walking on the open terrain 8-13 fps; 05:02:20 a shortcut to the terminal entrance, then 29 fps drawn, 58 on screen. The card computes fully in both (99-100 %, 236-250 W at full clock), so not heat or a slowed clock; the memory stays at 7.7 of 8.2 GB. The measuring run must wait for a switch and log both states (passes, shadow pages, memory, sky capture, what is open on screen); live weather held still by a fixed theme. Looking straight up (pitch 60-90) was 160-185 ms, which speaks against the far ground, mountains and grass. Then measure with the sky atmosphere, the volumetric fog, the sky light's real-time capture and the bloom off in turn. The game's log names no pass: log the graphics card's time per pass (stat GPU numbers) into ADP-Perf.log for bad frames, so this is seen without guessing. Goes together with 256 (apron and gates slow outdoors, passenger side fine indoors: the same cause may fit); also check why the wash hose is made ready four times in the log.", '', ['none'], '-', False),
    ('267', "High beams very much wider on every vehicle, and reaching a little further forward; NOT whiter (owner, 0.18.2, 2026-11-25, in the fire truck at night): high beams also throw light out to the sides. Now (FireTruck.cpp 758-775, GSE.cpp BuildBeams) the high beam is one narrow cone per side, 30 degrees wide with a 12 degree core, tint 1.0/0.96/0.9 (slightly warm), next to a low beam 72 degrees wide. New: the far core reaches a little further forward, and a very wide, softer spread is added per side (about 100-120 degrees, clearly wider than the low beam, checked against photos of real headlamps), lighting the verges, signs and the walls beside the road. The colour stays as it is. The fire truck's numbers are in Content/Settings/TruckLamps.ini (its note says full width, the code takes it as half: correct the note); the ground vehicles' are in the code. The same for the tugs, all service vehicles, coaches and taxis, for you and hired crew; no lens flares, no shadows added, the frame cost measured (256).", '', ['none'], '-', False),
    ('268', "The fire station's outside wall lamps give light at night (owner, 0.18.2, 2026-11-25): the lamps on its walls, meant to shine down, light nothing. Cause: Data/babylon-firestation-lights.json holds only six lamps, all in the hall's ceiling (hl, height 8.3 m); the lamps on the outside walls are only modelled, no light behind them. Give every outside wall lamp its light, its place read from the model, pointing down: a gentle, normal pool on the ground in front of the garage doors (and along the walls where the lamps hang), a little less bright than the terminal's apron, never washed out, no hard edge on the wall, and the lamp's own lens glowing to match. On at dusk and off after sunrise like the apron masts (LightMasts.cpp), same warmth as the station's own lamps, through the lamp system with a distance limit, no flares, no shadows; the frame cost measured there (266). Checked at night with the doors open and closed, dry and wet.", '', ['none'], '-', False),
    ('269', "ALL PERFORMANCE IN ONE GO (owner, 2026-11-25; said again the same day: the bad frames when looking in certain directions are super bad, keep them in mind, so they come first in the round): every frame rate report is tested and fixed together in one measuring round, not one by one: 256 (gates and apron), 266 (fire station, switching between good and bad, frame generation stopping), 257 (stalls after shortcuts), 269's walkway below, 82 and 195 (clouds), 76 (graphics memory), 253 (tablet lag), the 50 ms stutter once a minute, frame generation sometimes adding no pictures, 259 (wet apron). New report, photo of 2026-11-25 09:35 game time: on the Floor 2 gate walkway under the glass roof, looking along it, the frames are bad and in another direction good; it was not like this before. From the owner's log of that time: 05:09:48-05:10:18 UTC 18 fps drawn, graphics card 54 ms, frame generation adding 1 picture a second (3 %); the half minute before 33 fps, 29 ms; the half minutes 05:04-05:06 inside 50 fps, 19 ms. First suspects because they came with 0.18.2 and were not in 0.18.1: commits b864ce7f and 7a861971 (2026-11-23): the sky light on for good (before that its real-time capture stayed black and gave nothing: now it catches the sky with its clouds), the clouds' coverage and density per weather new (broken clouds now really open, so the rays travel further), the terminal's glass made new and mirroring more, the wave roof mirrored whole in the panes. The photo's view looks along that glass roof into the sky. In 2026-11-17 the same was found already (82): clear sky showing clouds under 20 fps, without clouds 50-60. Method: packaged 0.18.2 against 0.18.1 at the same fixed views (walkway both ways, apron to the terminal, gates west and east, fire station inside, in the doorway and outside, terminal entrance, open terrain, Grand Hall), day and night, clear and broken with live weather held, 3840 x 1080, frame generation on and off; then one suspect off at a time (clouds, sky light, glass, reflections, volumetric fog, grass, trees), each time per pass, with graphics memory and the good/bad switch logged. Wins without visible loss are made; anything visible only with the owner's word. Every result in docs/Performance-Log.md, then the full stress walk.", '', ['none'], '-', False),
    ('270', "EMERGENCY EXIT DOORS: STAFF AND THE PLAYER WALK THROUGH, PASSENGERS NEVER (the owner's report and photo, 2026-11-25, 0.18.2): the owner cannot go through the emergency exit doors on Floor 2 (the log has them by the one at x 234.5, z 24). CAUSE (read in the model scripts, not run): the 16 double doors there (9 in the south wall ESC-S-01..09, 7 to the walkway ESC-N-01..07) are one solid model each, east_c_escdoor, frame and both leaves together; nothing opens them. TO BUILD: frame and two leaves as own parts, each leaf with its hinge and its push bar; opened by hand with E / X from both sides, with the outline glow (the standing rule), the hand really on the push bar, the bar going in, the leaf swinging out of the person's way and shutting by itself, never in front of anyone still coming through; the sign and the frame stay. The player's own person and all staff (terminal, ground crew, police, fire, cleaners ...) use them, and staff may take them as a shorter way. Passengers never, not with a queue, a delay or a lost way: the doors are not in their walking map, and one held open for staff is still shut to them. Look at all emergency exits of the airport in the same go (the 11 closed escape stairs ESC-01..11 with their own doors, the ground lobbies' D-F1-RAMP-<g>B-1, the arrivals hall's, the one in the glass wall at the passport control): the same rule for each, and behind each a floor or stairs to stand on, no drop. Check that no visitor wall of the security line can be walked round by passengers through them. No alarm (owner, 2026-11-25): silent for the player and the staff.", '', ['none'], '-', False),
    ('271', "THE GATE AGENT'S DESK AT EVERY GATE: CHAIR, COMPUTER AND WHAT IT DOES (the owner's report and photo, 2026-11-25, 0.18.2; with to-do 210 and 244's gate agents). TODAY: every gate has a plain box desk, a block chair and a dark slab floating on the top as its screen (GATE-DESK / OFFICE-CHAIR of the old terminal model, copied to the east gates). (1) THE CHAIR: a very high quality office chair (base with five castors, gas lift, padded seat and back, armrests, stitching), own model from reference photos, blueprint first; it turns and rolls, sittable with the sitting IK by the gate agent and the player. (2) THE FLOATING SLAB GOES at every gate. (3) THE COMPUTER, the same quality as the one in the owner's office: a high quality monitor on a real stand, a keyboard and a mouse on the desk (cable or wireless, hands on them by IK); no computer case to be seen, it is inside the desk. Desk top with the stone stays; the desk itself is not changed unless the owner says so. Everything instanced + Nanite, at every gate, gates 1-16. (4) THE GATE PROGRAM on that screen, native C++ (no web layer), used with E / X (outline glow) sitting or standing, mouse or gamepad, by the player and by hired gate agents through the same functions, limits and speed: (4a) THE JETWAY CORRIDOR DOOR of that gate: open, close, and choose stay open or stay closed (a third choice, automatic, is what the crew have today: it opens on its own with a gate agent on shift); the screen shows the door's real state, it never closes on anyone, and a swing gate's other door set stays locked (214). (4b) THE FLIGHT, everything the other boarding and schedule screens show, from the same data: flight, destination, aircraft, stand, times and delays, boarding state and groups, seats sold / checked in / through security / at the gate / boarded / missing, bags. (4c) THE PASSENGER LIST: every passenger of the flight by name, the same persons as in the 3D world and the tracking map (FADPPassenger: name, flight, seat, stage), never invented separately: seat and class, where they are going (and the connection, if they have one), where they are now, checked in, boarded, bags, passport check done where the flight needs it; search, sort, filter; a chosen passenger's page. The person standing in front of the desk is picked on the list by the outline. (4d) THEIR RECORD on that page: bad conduct at this airport and aboard before (warnings, security or police called, removed, refused, from what really happened in the world, to-do 69), and, instead of a jail past, ONLY A POLICE FLAG (owner, 2026-11-25: flagged or not, no jail time and no reason shown). NOT IN THE GAME YET: a passenger has only the one mark that security or police were called now (bConduct); to be built is a kept record per person (saved with the career, the same person comes back with the same record), most with none, a small share with a fitting history, name, age and reason consistent. (4e) THE DECISION per passenger: allow / refuse boarding, changeable until the door shuts, shown to the crew; a refused one is told at the desk, answers in their own way (calm, or 69's conduct scene: talk, security, police), their bag is taken off the aircraft (no bag flies without its owner), the seat count and the flight's numbers follow, a refused traveller walks back out; a refusal without reason costs the airport (complaint, compensation), one with a good reason does not, and letting a dangerous one aboard can have its result later. Hired gate agents decide by their own skill and a rule the owner sets (always allow / refuse at bad conduct / ask me). REALITY, for the owner: a real gate agent sees the airline's own notes (earlier bad conduct, refused before, no-fly list of that airline) and what the border or police have flagged, not a person's jail past; only the police know that. Built as the owner asked (jail shown), unless the owner wants it realistic, with the past only as a police flag. BLUEPRINT FIRST (chair, monitor, keyboard, mouse; and the program's pages), the owner's OK, then built.", '', ['none'], '-', False),
    ('272', "THE TREES WITH THEIR REAL LEAVES AND NEEDLES, FULL NANITE, ON THE WHOLE SCENERY (the owner's report and two photos, 2026-11-25, 0.18.2): the trees by the airport are green lumps on bare poles and the conifers scaly cones; the owner wants the actual leaves and needles on them, the full Nanite trees the owner downloaded, and the same on the entire mountain and scenery. CAUSE (read, not run): the 1,811,031 trees round Chablais (SiteTrees.cpp, to-do 200) are our own five simple models of 2026-11-21 (Art/Trees/make_trees.py: Spruce_A/B, Fir_A, Beech_A, BeechBare_A): solid lumps without leaf cards, a tiling leaf or needle picture on them; they were never the downloaded ones. The only downloaded trees in the game are Epic's Nanite European quaking aspens (the Procedural Vegetation plugin of 5.7, four shapes, 131 on the forecourt's island); its other sample there is a common hazel, a bush. The photos' trunks also show untextured, pale and straight. TO BUILD: (1) FIRST look at what the owner has downloaded (Fab / Epic's Nanite trees; the owner, 2026-11-25: added to their Epic account, place on disk unknown, so look in the Epic launcher's Fab library), check the licence for a game that is sold (THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES) and which kinds are there; wanted for the Rhone valley at Chablais: Norway spruce, silver fir, larch, Scots pine, beech, oak, ash, lime / maple, birch, poplar, and bushes; several shapes, ages and sizes of each; kinds not to be had are made by us to the same quality, never left as lumps. (2) real trunk, branches, twigs and single leaves / needles as geometry (Nanite foliage: assemblies and voxels far away in 5.7, no see-through cards), bark with its real texture, two-sided leaf shading with the sun through the leaves, no two neighbours alike (turn, size, tint). (3) the same trees at every distance, the mountains' forests included: the 4 km limit round you must not show as a ring; far forest keeps its shape and colour; positions, heights and crown widths stay those of to-do 200 (only where real trees are). (4) seasons and snow as decided (beeches green all year for now; snow only with snow on the ground), wind sway measured first (143). (5) the near trees block walking and driving. (6) MEASURED: frames before and after at the fixed views of 269, graphics memory and the size of the download; 1.8 million trees is the risk, so it is tried on one block (1 km) first and the numbers shown to the owner before it goes everywhere; performance by cheaper far trees, never by fewer. Ocean and lake shores, hedges and the town's trees follow the same rule (aspens stay unless the owner says otherwise).", '', ['none'], '-', False),
    ('273', "THE DIRT SHINES WHITE IN THE SUN (the owner's report and photo, 2026-11-25, 0.18.2): the soil between the grass blades glares white toward the sun (looking over the grass at the building with the red tower). The owner: the ground should not receive more than 3% light; only the dirt / soil, NOT the pavements, those stay exactly as they are. READING: the dirt's shine (what it mirrors of sun and sky) at most 3%, matte in every light; its own daylight colour stays (owner, 2026-11-25: only the shine at 3%). CAUSE (read, most likely, not run): MI_ggrass and the terminal model's lawn are instances of the one master ground M_Ground (Tools/set_ground.py) with the paving's wet rule: wet makes everything smooth (roughness to 0.28) and specular 0.3, so damp soil shines like wet asphalt, worst against a low sun; after rain it lasts while the ground dries (3 hours). TO DO: a switch on the master for soil and grass: no wet gloss (wet soil only gets darker, roughness stays 0.95 or more), specular down so the shine is at most 3%, dry and wet, at low sun and in lamp light, no puddle mirror on it unless it is a real puddle; also the same check for the Chablais photo ground (the globe's and the land corridor's, which is already matte) and the soft bands where paving meets soil. Pavements, paint and puddles on paving unchanged (259 has the wet apron). The owner's earlier vivid sunsets on the paving stay. With it, look at whether the grass is too thin there: far more soil shows in the photo than a real meadow's; a denser cover from the grass slider as it stands, nothing lowered. Check by eye at noon, low sun, after rain and at night, and frames at the same spot (a glossy ground may cost reflections, with 256 / 259 / 266).", '', ['none'], '-', False),
    ('274', "THE MAIN HALL: HANGING GLOBE LAMPS INSTEAD OF THE ROOF'S LIGHT SPOTS (the owner's wish and photo, 2026-11-25): every light spot in the roof over the main hall (the Grand Hall, x 36..114, inside the glass) goes and one of the white globe lamps hangs in its place, at exactly the same position in plan (Tools/wave_roof.py downlights(): a 6 m grid; 53 of them are inside). The lamps are a COPY: the originals (a white globe on a thin black cord: the Floor 2 bar's ten F2-BAR/GLOBE-LIGHT, 40 cm, and the lounge's six, 34 cm) stay exactly as they are everywhere else; the hall's own model is 2.5 TIMES as big, so 1.0 m across (the bar's 40 cm x 2.5; not 2.5 m unless the owner says so). In the photo the globe's outline shows straight edges: the hall's copy gets a smooth round globe, opal glass lit from inside, with its small holder on top, cord and a ceiling rose where it meets the wave roof (the cord straight down, its top following the roof's height). How low they hang: the roof is 14-21 m up there; they follow the roof's wave (owner, 2026-11-25), every cord the same length, the length shown in a picture first; at the least clear of the Floor 2 landing's heads, the escalators, the tree pillars' arms, the glass walls, the boards and the hanging signs: one that would touch any of them is listed, not silently moved. They really light the hall: soft warm-white light through the lamp system with a distance limit, no flares, dusk to sunrise as the spots' roof light today (SoffitLights: 42 panels stay the base, measured so the hall is not brighter than now or washed out), glow without bloom hiding the shape. REMOVED: only the spots inside the main hall; the spotlights outside under the roof's overhang stay (owner, 2026-11-25); those over the other halls and the promenade stay too. One instanced Nanite mesh, no z-fighting, the materials' empty slots checked; frames measured with 269.", '', ['none'], '-', False),
    ('275', "THE TIMETABLES SHOW YOUR CAREER'S REAL FLIGHTS, AND OTHER AIRLINES COME ONLY AFTER A FORMAL REQUEST (owner, 2026-11-25). Every departures and arrivals board and every gate screen shows the actual flights of the career being played: your own aircraft (flight number, place, scheduled and real time, delays, gate, belt, status as it happens) and the aircraft of other airlines that fly to your airport. Other airlines don't come by themselves any more: each first sends you a formal request (airline, aircraft type, route, days and times, the stand/gate and handling it needs, what it pays); you accept it, or deny it and give a reason (no free gate or slot, aircraft too large, too little staff or equipment, noise or night hours, unsafe airline, too low a fee, competition with your own route ...). Only an accepted one gets its slot, appears on the boards and flies in. A denied one gets your answer in writing and may ask again later with better terms. Native C++, saved with the career.", '', ['none'], '-', False),
    ('276', "NO STUCK PROMPT WHILE SITTING, AND WALKING KEYS STAND YOU UP (owner, 2026-11-25). The prompt that frees you never shows while you sit on any chair or seat in the terminal or in a vehicle, climb into one, or are carried by one. W A S D or the left stick pressed while you sit makes you stand up, the same as E.", '', ['none'], '-', False),
    ('277', "THE REMOTE STANDS R0 TO R7 IN THE GAME (owner, 2026-11-25). The eight remote stands of the approved plan, built into the airfield: pavement, stand markings and numbers, lights, the taxi ways to them, the stairs, buses and ground crew that serve them. BUILT BEFORE the performance run and the publish (owner, 2026-11-25). WHO STANDS WHERE (owner, 2026-11-25): an aircraft that fits the jetways goes to a gate with a jetway; else to a gate without one, where the passengers walk or are taken by airport bus, whichever is best for the distance to walk; the remote stands take the A320, the like of it and larger, and only when there is no room at the gates, or when told to go there (tower, player). From a remote stand the passengers ALWAYS go by bus: it drives up to a ground gate in front of the terminal and lets them out there, so they come in that way; departing passengers the other way round. Light masts at the stands; the large stands' tails lit too. BUSES (owner, 2026-11-25): also at a ground gate at the terminal, when the passengers' aircraft stands further away than right at their gate; always for a remote stand. STAIRS: aircraft stairs are there and used. No cabin yet: boarding, the passengers walk up the stairs and one by one, as each reaches the top, disappear; arriving, the first one comes out at the top of the stairs, walks down and gets into the bus, the others after them; the bus drives off when all of them are in. THE BUS ITSELF (owner, 2026-11-25): the apron bus's finished model imported with its livery and electric sounds; hands and feet on the real parts (IK) for the driver, for passengers sitting and holding the poles, and for the player; the player drives it with every control, as the crew do; and the player can ride along whenever it stops to take passengers: walks in by its doors, stands or sits anywhere, holds on, gets out where it stops (to a remote stand or back). VEHICLE AREA (owner, 2026-11-25, also before the performance run): besides the vehicles in front of the gates, an area at the remote stands where buses, stairs, pushback tugs, GPUs and the other airport vehicles are kept: those bought beyond what is needed or what the gates can hold, and spares, as vehicles can break (sometimes repairable, sometimes not). The airport buses are electric: they charge there; if it has no room, a place of its own for charging. Every electric vehicle goes a real distance on one charge, and the ground crew see to it that they are charged in time.", '', ['none'], '-', False),
    ('278', "SHADOW QUEUE OVERFLOW (testing issue; owner: fix it, 2026-11-25): the log warns that the virtual shadow maps' marking job queue for non-Nanite objects overflows: find which objects cause it, fix it without lowering the shadows' quality.", '', ['none'], '-', False),
    ('279', "CHARACTER CREATOR CUT OFF ON ULTRAWIDE (32:9) (testing issue; owner: fix it, 2026-11-25): the whole creator visible and usable on every screen shape.", '', ['none'], '-', False),
    ('280', "BLOCKED PATH AT THE ROTUNDA (testing issue; owner: fix it, 2026-11-25): find what blocks the walkway at the rotunda and free it for passengers and the player.", '', ['none'], '-', False),
    ('281', "LIVE WEATHER OVERRIDES THE TEST WEATHER BAR (testing issue; owner: fix it, 2026-11-25): weather set on the test bar stays until it is set back to live.", '', ['none'], '-', False),
    ('282', "THE MENUS' WEATHER REQUESTS ARE BLOCKED (testing issue; owner: fix it, 2026-11-25): the main menu's weather comes through again (the browser layer's cross-site block); fetched natively in C++.", '', ['none'], '-', False),
    ('283', "ONE CONTROL, TWO JOBS (testing issue; owner: fix it, 2026-11-25): left thumbstick (horn), H and D-pad down are each used for two things at once: every control does one thing only, the key help shows the new ones.", '', ['none'], '-', False),
    ('284', "TUG DOOR SOUND MISSING (testing issue; owner: fix it, 2026-11-25): the sound TugDoorOpen isn't found: the door gets its sound.", '', ['none'], '-', False),
    ('285', "PASSENGERS AND CREW MISSING IN THE PUBLISHED GAMES (found 2026-11-25): 0.18.0, 0.18.1 and 0.18.2 were packaged with 8 of the 144 people, so almost nobody walked into the terminal. All of them go into the package now, the release stops if one is missing, and each new person is loaded in the background so that nobody's arrival makes the picture stand still.", 'written, not in a package yet', ['none'], '-', False),
    ('286', "REAL TIMETABLES ON THE BIG VERTICAL SCREENS IN THE MAIN HALL (owner, 2026-11-25; before the next publish if it fits). The tall departure and arrival totems show the actual timetable, never a sample one: with no flight connected to the airport the timetable is still shown (title, clock, column heads, empty rows); as soon as an aircraft, the player's own or an AI airline's, has a connection to the airport (a planned or running departure or arrival) it appears as in the real world. No board in the terminal shows sample flights any more. PAGES (owner, 2026-11-25): more flights than fit on the screen are shown page after page, round and round as in real airports (10-15 s a page, the time looked up in real airports' standards when built; 'Page 1/3' shown; all screens of a kind turn together; flights gone from the list leave at the next turn); with one page nothing turns. The drawing moves from the web layer to native C++.", '', ['none'], '-', False),
    ('287', "PEOPLE SUNK IN THE PAVEMENT, ROAD AND CROSSINGS OUTSIDE THE MAIN HALL (owner, 2026-11-25). Cause: in a packaged game the five roads with their pavements and crossings have no solid surface (Unreal makes one only in the editor), so people walk on the ground underneath. The roads are made solid as they are built, and anyone below a road comes up onto it.", 'proven in the outside run 2026-11-25: everyone on top in every check', ['none'], '-', False),
    ('288', "ANNOUNCER: WRONG CALLS (testing issue, found 2026-11-25 in the code): a cancelled flight can still get its boarding, final and closed calls; a flight whose passengers walk here gets two final calls; the hall's announcer calls the gate closed 15 minutes before departure while the timetable still says boarding or final call until 10.", 'heard in a run 2026-11-25: calls, gate and boards agree', ['none'], '-', False),
    ('289', "ALL THE ANNOUNCEMENTS A REAL AIRPORT MAKES (owner, 2026-11-25): check-in, gate and gate changes, delays with their reason, buses to remote stands, arrivals, baggage belts, weather, airport closed, safety, found things, drivers, rooms and shops. STANDING: every later feature brings its announcements (one table, UI/AirportCalls). Later, owner's choice: French or German after the English.", 'heard in a run 2026-11-25: calls, gate and boards agree', ['none'], '-', False),
